fad251bd-ece0-47da-920d-00014d92b2fb	c0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000003	Full Stack Developer	[{"id": "q1", "type": "MCQ", "skill": "React", "points": 10, "options": [{"id": "opt1", "text": "The parent is passing an inline arrow function or object literal as a prop without wrapping it in useCallback or useMemo."}, {"id": "opt2", "text": "The child component is using Tailwind CSS utility classes, which bypass React's virtual DOM diffing."}, {"id": "opt3", "text": "React.memo is incompatible with TypeScript interfaces and only works with type aliases."}, {"id": "opt4", "text": "The parent component is missing a key prop in its own rendering lifecycle."}], "question": "In a React application built with TypeScript, you notice a child component is re-rendering unnecessarily on every parent state update, even though its props haven't changed. The child component is wrapped in React.memo. Which of the following is the most likely cause?", "difficulty": "EASY", "explanation": "React.memo performs a shallow comparison of props. If the parent passes an inline function or object, a new reference is created on every render, causing the shallow comparison to fail and triggering a re-render of the child. Wrapping the function in useCallback or the object in useMemo preserves the reference.", "correctOptionId": "opt1"}, {"id": "q2", "type": "MCQ", "skill": "System Architecture", "points": 10, "options": [{"id": "opt1", "text": "Implementing a GraphQL API to fetch nested resources in a single query."}, {"id": "opt2", "text": "Using Tailwind CSS's arbitrary variants to lazy-load the posts."}, {"id": "opt3", "text": "Storing all posts in local storage on the initial application load."}, {"id": "opt4", "text": "Converting the database from PostgreSQL to MongoDB."}], "question": "You are designing a dashboard that needs to fetch a list of users and, for each user, their latest 3 posts. Using a standard REST API design, you might encounter the 'N+1 query problem' on the client side. Which architectural approach or pattern best mitigates this issue without making multiple round-trips?", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "explanation": "GraphQL allows clients to define the exact shape of the data they need, including nested relationships (users and their posts), in a single request. This directly solves the client-side N+1 request problem.", "correctOptionId": "opt1"}, {"id": "q3", "type": "MCQ", "skill": "Tailwind", "points": 10, "options": [{"id": "opt1", "text": "Tailwind is dynamically generating classes at runtime instead of during the build step."}, {"id": "opt2", "text": "The 'content' array in tailwind.config.js is misconfigured or missing, preventing the compiler from purging unused classes."}, {"id": "opt3", "text": "You are using TypeScript instead of JavaScript, which doubles the CSS output."}, {"id": "opt4", "text": "Figma styles were imported directly as raw SVG strings in the HTML."}], "question": "When deploying a production build of a React + Tailwind CSS application, you notice the CSS bundle size is unexpectedly large (several megabytes). What is the most likely reason for this?", "difficulty": "EASY", "explanation": "Tailwind CSS relies on static analysis of your source files to find used classes and purge unused ones. If the 'content' array in tailwind.config.js is not pointing to your source files (e.g., components, pages), Tailwind cannot purge unused styles, resulting in the entire default utility library being bundled.", "correctOptionId": "opt2"}, {"id": "q4", "type": "THEORY", "skill": "System Architecture", "points": 15, "rubric": "Look for clear definitions of CSR, SSR, and SSG, along with trade-offs regarding SEO, server load, and initial page load speed.", "question": "Explain the differences between Client-Side Rendering (CSR), Server-Side Rendering (SSR), and Static Site Generation (SSG). Under what scenarios would you choose SSR over CSR for a React-based application, considering SEO and initial load performance?", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "expectedTopics": ["Client-Side Rendering (CSR)", "Server-Side Rendering (SSR)", "Static Site Generation (SSG)", "SEO", "Time to Interactive (TTI)", "First Contentful Paint (FCP)"], "referenceAnswer": "CSR renders everything in the browser using JS, leading to slower initial paint but fast subsequent transitions. SSR renders HTML on the server for every request, improving SEO and FCP. SSG pre-renders HTML at build time, offering the best performance but is unsuitable for highly dynamic data. Choose SSR when content changes frequently, needs to be highly dynamic, and SEO is critical (e.g., e-commerce product pages)."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "THEORY", "skill": "System Architecture", "points": 15, "rubric": "Evaluate the candidate's understanding of data structures. A document store (NoSQL) is highly suitable for nested, polymorphic shape data, whereas SQL is better for user accounts and permissions. A hybrid approach is also a strong answer.", "question": "You are tasked with designing the backend for a collaborative design tool (similar to Figma) where users can create canvases, place shapes, and collaborate in real-time. Would you choose a Relational Database (SQL) or a Document Store (NoSQL) to store the canvas state (shapes, coordinates, colors)? Explain your architectural reasoning.", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "expectedTopics": ["SQL vs NoSQL", "Document Store", "Schema flexibility", "Real-time updates", "JSON serialization"], "referenceAnswer": "A document store (like MongoDB) or a key-value store is highly suitable for canvas state because shapes have highly polymorphic schemas (e.g., a circle has a radius, a rectangle has width/height, paths have arrays of coordinates). Storing this as JSON documents allows fast read/write operations and flexible schemas. However, relational databases (SQL) are preferred for structured relational data like user accounts, billing, and workspace permissions."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "BEHAVIORAL", "skill": "Problem Solving", "points": 10, "rubric": "Look for proactive communication, empathy towards the designer, proposing alternative simplified solutions (MVP), and aligning with business goals.", "question": "Imagine a UI/UX designer hands you a Figma design for a complex, interactive dashboard widget that includes non-standard animations and layout structures. Implementing this exactly as designed would take you two weeks and delay the product release. How do you handle this situation?", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "expectedTopics": ["Collaboration", "Trade-offs", "Communication", "MVP (Minimum Viable Product)", "Technical feasibility"], "referenceAnswer": "I would schedule a quick sync with the designer to understand the core user experience goal of the widget. I would explain the technical constraints and the 2-week timeline impact. I would then propose a simplified alternative using standard Tailwind transitions that achieves 90% of the UX value in 2 days, agreeing to phase the complex animations in a future iteration."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "CODE", "skill": "TS/JS", "points": 20, "examples": [{"input": "cache.set('user_1', { name: 'Alice' }, 100); cache.get('user_1');", "output": "{ name: 'Alice' }", "explanation": "Retrieving the key immediately returns the cached object."}], "question": "Implement an in-memory cache utility class in TypeScript that can be used in a Full-Stack Node.js backend or a React frontend to cache API responses. The cache must support setting a key with a value and a Time-To-Live (TTL) in milliseconds, and retrieving the value only if it hasn't expired.", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "constraints": ["ttlMs > 0", "Keys are strings, values can be any type.", "Expired keys must return null."], "description": "Write a class `InMemoryCache` with methods `set(key, value, ttlMs)` and `get(key)`. Expired keys must return `null`.", "starterCode": "class InMemoryCache {\\n  private cache = new Map<string, { value: any; expiry: number }>();\\n\\n  set(key: string, value: any, ttlMs: number): void {\\n    // Your code here\\n  }\\n\\n  get(key: string): any | null {\\n    // Your code here\\n  }\\n}", "defaultLanguage": "TypeScript", "languageOptions": ["TypeScript", "JavaScript"], "expectedConcepts": ["Map / Hash table", "Timestamps (Date.now())", "Cache Expiration"]}]	[{"questionId": "q1", "selectedOptionId": "opt1"}, {"questionId": "q2", "selectedOptionId": "opt1"}, {"questionId": "q3", "selectedOptionId": "opt2"}, {"answerText": "Overview of Rendering Strategies\\nClient-Side Rendering (CSR): The server sends a minimal HTML shell with a JavaScript bundle. The browser downloads and executes the JavaScript, fetches API data, and dynamically constructs the DOM. Initial loads can be slow and heavily dependent on device performance, and search engine crawlers may struggle to index content if it isn't pre-rendered.\\n\\nServer-Side Rendering (SSR): Each incoming request triggers the server to fetch data, render the React components into an HTML string, and send the fully formed markup to the client. The browser immediately displays the content while hydrating the application to make it interactive.\\n\\nStatic Site Generation (SSG): Pages are pre-rendered at build time into static HTML files. These files can be cached and served instantly via Content Delivery Networks (CDNs), offering unmatched speed and reliability.\\n\\nChoosing SSR over CSR\\nServer-Side Rendering should be prioritized over Client-Side Rendering when dealing with applications that require strict SEO compliance and optimal initial load performance under specific conditions:\\n\\nSearch Engine Optimization (SEO): If public search visibility is critical—such as in e-commerce product pages, marketing sites, or blogs—SSR ensures that search engine crawlers receive a fully populated HTML document on the initial request, eliminating reliance on client-side JS execution for indexing.\\n\\nFast First Contentful Paint (FCP) on Slow Networks: SSR eliminates the blank loading state typical of CSR by delivering rendered HTML immediately. Users see content faster, lowering bounce rates even on constrained connections.\\n\\nDynamic, User-Specific Data Required on Every Request: Unlike SSG (which is ideal for static or infrequently updated content), SSR handles pages that require fresh, personalized, or authenticated data on every request (e.g., a personalized user dashboard or a real-time inventory tracker) without sacrificing initial render speed.\\n\\nTrade-Offs\\nServer Load & Costs: SSR requires a running Node.js server (or serverless functions) to compute responses for every request, increasing infrastructure complexity and hosting costs compared to static file hosting.\\n\\nTime to Interactive (TTI) Delays: While FCP is faster, users may experience a delay before the page becomes fully interactive if the JavaScript bundle is large and hydration takes longer to complete.", "questionId": "q4"}, {"answerText": "### Architecture Choice: Document Store (NoSQL)\\n\\nFor storing canvas state (shapes, coordinates, colors, dynamic attributes) in a collaborative design tool like Figma, a **Document Store (NoSQL)** (such as MongoDB, DynamoDB, or PostgreSQL with native JSONB) is the superior architectural choice over a strict Relational Database model.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Architectural Reasoning\\n\\n* **Schema Flexibility & Dynamic Object Models:**\\nDesign elements (rectangles, vectors, text, complex grouped shapes, frame constraints) have wildly varying properties. A relational schema would require either a bloated table with many nullable columns or complex EAV (Entity-Attribute-Value) anti-patterns. A document store allows each element/canvas state to be stored as a self-contained, polymorphic JSON document.\\n* **Natural Serialization & High-Throughput Reads/Writes:**\\nCanvas states directly match the object tree in memory on the client side. Storing canvas state as JSON documents eliminates complex ORM mapping overhead. The backend can serialize and deserialize canvas trees directly, reducing memory overhead and CPU cycles during frequent synchronization bursts.\\n* **Real-Time Updates & Granular Delta Sync:**\\nCollaborative tools process thousands of micro-updates per minute (e.g., updating dynamic coordinates during a drag operation). Document stores allow efficient atomic updates to specific nested attributes or arrays (using paths like `shapes.elementId.position.x`) without locking an entire relational row or table.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Trade-Offs\\n\\n* **Lack of Multi-Document ACID Transactions:** Relational databases provide strong ACID guarantees across multiple tables. With document stores, cross-document transactions are costlier, meaning data integrity (e.g., canvas metadata vs. element state) must be handled at the application layer or within single-document atomicity limits.\\n* **Storage Redundancy & Eventual Consistency:** Document models often favor data denormalization for read/write performance, which can lead to larger storage footprints and require conflict-resolution algorithms (such as CRDTs or OT) for real-time multiplayer consistency.", "questionId": "q5"}, {"answerText": "### Assessing Technical Feasibility & Impact\\n\\n* **Analyze the Bottleneck:** Immediately evaluate why the design takes two weeks—whether it stems from custom, non-standard animations, complex layout hierarchies, or missing component primitives.\\n* **Scope vs. Value Trade-off:** Weigh the high implementation cost against the user value delivered by those specific custom animations. Often, standard UI primitives or lightweight alternatives deliver 90% of the UX value in a fraction of the time.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Collaborative Problem-Solving & Phased Delivery (MVP)\\n\\n* **Engage the Designer Early:** Schedule a quick sync with the designer to review technical constraints transparently. Frame the conversation around shipping value on time rather than outright rejection.\\n* **Propose a Phased Rollout (Phased MVP):**\\n* **Phase 1 (MVP for Release):** Deliver a streamlined version of the widget using standard layout components and native or lightweight transitions that meet the core functional requirements and hit the product release deadline.\\n* **Phase 2 (Enhancement):** Queue the custom, non-standard animations and complex polish items for a fast-follow patch or subsequent sprint once the release is stabilized.", "questionId": "q6"}, {"answerText": "class InMemoryCache<T = any> {\\n  private cache: Map<string, { value: T; expiry: number }> = new Map();\\n\\n  set(key: string, value: T, ttlMs: number): void {\\n    const expiry = Date.now() + ttlMs;\\n    this.cache.set(key, { value, expiry });\\n  }\\n\\n  get(key: string): T | null {\\n    const item = this.cache.get(key);\\n    \\n    if (!item) {\\n      return null;\\n    }\\n\\n    if (Date.now() > item.expiry) {\\n      this.cache.delete(key);\\n      return null;\\n    }\\n\\n    return item.value;\\n  }\\n}", "questionId": "q7"}]	100	Strong performance! You scored 100% overall (MCQ: 100%, Theory: 100%, Coding: 100%), passing the required threshold of 70%.	100	Strong performance! You scored 100% overall (MCQ: 100%, Theory: 100%, Coding: 100%), passing the required threshold of 70%.	{"passed": true, "summary": "Strong performance! You scored 100% overall (MCQ: 100%, Theory: 100%, Coding: 100%), passing the required threshold of 70%.", "mcqScore": 100, "strengths": ["Understands React", "Understands System Architecture", "Understands Tailwind", "Clear definitions of all three rendering strategies.", "Accurate identification of key performance metrics (FCP, TTI).", "Pragmatic discussion of infrastructure costs and hydration delays.", "Strong justification based on schema flexibility and polymorphic data.", "Identified the performance benefits of direct JSON serialization.", "Acknowledged critical trade-offs like ACID transactions and consistency models (CRDT/OT).", "Proactive and collaborative approach to working with the designer.", "Clear strategy for phased delivery (MVP first, polish later).", "Focuses on delivering business value without outright rejecting creative designs.", "Proper use of TypeScript generics.", "Correct implementation of TTL logic using Date.now().", "Cleans up expired keys from memory during the get() operation."], "codingScore": 100, "skillScores": {"React": 100, "TS/JS": 100, "Tailwind": 100, "Problem Solving": 100, "System Architecture": 100}, "theoryScore": 100, "improvements": ["Could optionally implement an active cleanup mechanism (e.g., setInterval) to prevent memory leaks from expired keys that are never queried again."], "overallScore": 100, "questionEvaluations": [{"type": "MCQ", "verdict": "strong", "feedback": "Correct answer! React.memo performs a shallow comparison of props. If the parent passes an inline function or object, a new reference is created on every render, causing the shallow comparison to fail and triggering a re-render of the child. Wrapping the function in useCallback or the object in useMemo preserves the reference.", "isCorrect": true, "strengths": ["Understands React"], "questionId": "q1", "improvements": [], "pointsEarned": 10, "pointsPossible": 10}, {"type": "MCQ", "verdict": "strong", "feedback": "Correct answer! GraphQL allows clients to define the exact shape of the data they need, including nested relationships (users and their posts), in a single request. This directly solves the client-side N+1 request problem.", "isCorrect": true, "strengths": ["Understands System Architecture"], "questionId": "q2", "improvements": [], "pointsEarned": 10, "pointsPossible": 10}, {"type": "MCQ", "verdict": "strong", "feedback": "Correct answer! Tailwind CSS relies on static analysis of your source files to find used classes and purge unused ones. If the 'content' array in tailwind.config.js is not pointing to your source files (e.g., components, pages), Tailwind cannot purge unused styles, resulting in the entire default utility library being bundled.", "isCorrect": true, "strengths": ["Understands Tailwind"], "questionId": "q3", "improvements": [], "pointsEarned": 10, "pointsPossible": 10}, {"type": "THEORY", "verdict": "strong", "feedback": "The candidate provided an exceptionally clear and comprehensive explanation of CSR, SSR, and SSG. The reasoning for choosing SSR over CSR is technically sound, highlighting SEO benefits, FCP improvements, and dynamic data handling, while accurately identifying trade-offs like server load and TTI.", "strengths": ["Clear definitions of all three rendering strategies.", "Accurate identification of key performance metrics (FCP, TTI).", "Pragmatic discussion of infrastructure costs and hydration delays."], "questionId": "q4", "improvements": [], "pointsEarned": 15, "pointsPossible": 15}, {"type": "THEORY", "verdict": "strong", "feedback": "Excellent architectural analysis. The candidate correctly identifies the polymorphic nature of canvas elements and argues convincingly for a Document Store (or hybrid JSONB approach). They also demonstrate deep domain knowledge by mentioning CRDTs/OT for real-time collaboration.", "strengths": ["Strong justification based on schema flexibility and polymorphic data.", "Identified the performance benefits of direct JSON serialization.", "Acknowledged critical trade-offs like ACID transactions and consistency models (CRDT/OT)."], "questionId": "q5", "improvements": [], "pointsEarned": 15, "pointsPossible": 15}, {"type": "BEHAVIORAL", "verdict": "strong", "feedback": "The candidate demonstrates excellent communication skills, empathy, and a business-oriented mindset. The proposal of a phased MVP approach is the industry-standard way to handle such design-to-development bottlenecks.", "strengths": ["Proactive and collaborative approach to working with the designer.", "Clear strategy for phased delivery (MVP first, polish later).", "Focuses on delivering business value without outright rejecting creative designs."], "questionId": "q6", "improvements": [], "pointsEarned": 10, "pointsPossible": 10}, {"type": "CODE", "verdict": "strong", "feedback": "The TypeScript implementation is clean, correct, and type-safe. It uses a Map for O(1) lookups and correctly implements lazy eviction of expired keys.", "strengths": ["Proper use of TypeScript generics.", "Correct implementation of TTL logic using Date.now().", "Cleans up expired keys from memory during the get() operation."], "questionId": "q7", "improvements": ["Could optionally implement an active cleanup mechanism (e.g., setInterval) to prevent memory leaks from expired keys that are never queried again."], "pointsEarned": 20, "pointsPossible": 20}], "recommendedNextSteps": ["Focus on improving: Could optionally implement an active cleanup mechanism (e.g., setInterval) to prevent memory leaks from expired keys that are never queried again."]}	2026-09-12 08:16:09.801+00	EVALUATED	1	\N	\N	PRACTICE	\N	1	100	100	100	{"React": 100, "TS/JS": 100, "Tailwind": 100, "Problem Solving": 100, "System Architecture": 100}	t	2026-09-12 08:16:09.801+00
015899c6-0da5-4c22-a81a-18bc704ebe71	c0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000003	Full Stack Developer	[{"id": "q1", "type": "MCQ", "skill": "React", "points": 10, "options": [{"id": "opt1", "text": "Store the stock prices in a global React Context and consume it in all components."}, {"id": "opt2", "text": "Isolate the real-time display into a leaf component, and use a state management library like Zustand or Redux with selective selectors, or wrap static components in React.memo."}, {"id": "opt3", "text": "Force-update the DOM directly using document.getElementById() inside a useEffect hook to bypass React's virtual DOM entirely."}, {"id": "opt4", "text": "Increase the WebSocket polling interval to 5000ms so that the re-renders happen less frequently."}], "question": "You are building a high-traffic dashboard in React that displays real-time stock prices. The parent component receives updates every 100ms via WebSockets. To prevent the entire component tree (including heavy static charts and navigation sidebars) from re-rendering on every update, which of the following is the most architecturally sound approach?", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "explanation": "React.memo prevents functional components from re-rendering if their props haven't changed. Alternatively, using a state management tool with fine-grained selectors (like Zustand) ensures only the components subscribing to the specific changing slice of state will re-render, isolating the performance impact of high-frequency updates.", "correctOptionId": "opt2"}, {"id": "q2", "type": "MCQ", "skill": "System Architecture", "points": 10, "options": [{"id": "opt1", "text": "Migrate the entire database from PostgreSQL to MongoDB to speed up query times."}, {"id": "opt2", "text": "Implement dynamic imports using React.lazy() and Suspense to split the bundle into route-based chunks."}, {"id": "opt3", "text": "Convert all Tailwind CSS classes into inline styles to reduce CSS file overhead."}, {"id": "opt4", "text": "Use Server-Sent Events (SSE) to stream the bundle file progressively to the client."}], "question": "A client complains that your React application loads slowly on mobile devices with poor network connections. Upon inspecting the network tab, you notice that the main bundle size is 4.5MB. Which architectural pattern should you implement first to solve this?", "difficulty": "EASY", "explanation": "Route-based code splitting using React.lazy and Suspense allows the browser to download only the JavaScript required for the current page, drastically reducing the initial load time and bundle size on slow networks.", "correctOptionId": "opt2"}, {"id": "q3", "type": "MCQ", "skill": "System Architecture", "points": 10, "options": [{"id": "opt1", "text": "Status: 200 OK; Header: Cache-Control: max-age=3600"}, {"id": "opt2", "text": "Status: 301 Moved Permanently; Header: Expires: 3600"}, {"id": "opt3", "text": "Status: 200 OK; Header: Keep-Alive: timeout=3600"}, {"id": "opt4", "text": "Status: 204 No Content; Header: Cache-Control: no-store"}], "question": "When designing a RESTful API for a blogging platform, which HTTP header and status code combination is most appropriate for instructing a client browser or CDN to cache a static article page for exactly 1 hour?", "difficulty": "EASY", "explanation": "The 'Cache-Control: max-age=3600' header tells the client and intermediate caches (like CDNs) that the response is fresh and can be cached for 3600 seconds (1 hour). Status 200 OK indicates a successful request returning content.", "correctOptionId": "opt1"}, {"id": "q4", "type": "THEORY", "skill": "System Architecture", "points": 15, "rubric": "The candidate should clearly define CSR, SSR, and SSG. They must evaluate each against SEO, speed, and server cost. Finally, they must recommend SSG (or SSR with caching) for product catalogs to maximize SEO/speed, and CSR for the checkout dashboard since it contains sensitive, user-specific dynamic data.", "question": "You are tasked with building an e-commerce platform. Explain the trade-offs between Client-Side Rendering (CSR), Server-Side Rendering (SSR), and Static Site Generation (SSG) regarding SEO, Initial Page Load Time (Time to Interactive), and Server Load. Which rendering strategy would you choose for the product catalog pages versus the user checkout dashboard?", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "expectedTopics": ["SEO (Search Engine Optimization)", "Time to Interactive (TTI) / First Contentful Paint (FCP)", "Server overhead / scaling costs", "Static Site Generation (SSG) for product catalog", "Client-Side Rendering (CSR) for dynamic/authenticated checkout"], "referenceAnswer": "CSR loads a minimal HTML shell and fetches data via JS in the browser. It has poor SEO and slower initial paint but low server load. SSR generates HTML on every request, offering excellent SEO and fast initial paint, but puts high load on the server. SSG generates HTML at build time, offering the fastest load times and excellent SEO with zero runtime server overhead, but is bad for highly dynamic data.\\n\\nFor Product Catalog Pages: SSG (with Incremental Static Regeneration if supported) is ideal because products don't change every second, and SEO and speed are critical for conversions.\\nFor User Checkout Dashboard: CSR is ideal because the page is highly dynamic, private to the logged-in user, requires no SEO, and relies heavily on client-side state interactions."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "THEORY", "skill": "System Architecture", "points": 15, "rubric": "Look for an understanding of network overhead (HTTP headers in polling vs lightweight WebSocket frames) and connection state management. The candidate should choose WebSockets for low-latency, high-frequency bi-directional communication.", "question": "Describe how you would design a notification system that alerts users in real-time when they receive a message. Compare Short Polling, Long Polling, and WebSockets. Under what conditions would you choose WebSockets over the other two?", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "expectedTopics": ["Short Polling overhead", "Long Polling connection holding", "WebSockets bi-directional persistent connection", "Network overhead and latency", "Scalability / Connection limits"], "referenceAnswer": "Short Polling involves the client repeatedly sending HTTP requests at fixed intervals. It is simple but highly inefficient, wasting server resources and bandwidth with empty responses.\\nLong Polling keeps the HTTP request open until the server has new data. It reduces latency compared to short polling but still incurs HTTP header overhead on every new connection.\\nWebSockets establish a single, persistent, bi-directional TCP connection. It has minimal overhead after the initial handshake.\\n\\nI would choose WebSockets when real-time, low-latency updates are critical (e.g., chat apps, collaborative tools) and when communication is frequent and bi-directional."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "BEHAVIORAL", "skill": "Problem Solving", "points": 10, "rubric": "Look for a collaborative mindset. The candidate should not just say 'no' to the design. They should explain how they would present the performance data, suggest lightweight alternatives (e.g., custom CSS/Tailwind animations instead of heavy JS libraries), and work together to find a compromise that preserves user experience without sacrificing performance.", "question": "Imagine a designer hands you a complex, highly interactive Figma design for a new dashboard. However, you notice that implementing the design exactly as-is will require loading several heavy third-party libraries, resulting in poor performance and a delay in the product launch. How would you handle this situation with the designer and product manager?", "difficulty": "EASY", "expectedTopics": ["Collaboration and communication", "Empathy for design goals", "Proposing alternative technical solutions", "Negotiation and compromise"], "referenceAnswer": "I would schedule a brief meeting with the designer and PM. First, I'd validate the design's aesthetic and user experience goals. Then, I would present the technical constraints objectively—showing how the heavy libraries would impact page load speed (especially on mobile) and delay the launch.\\n\\nInstead of just rejecting it, I would propose alternatives: for example, building a simplified version of the complex animation using native Tailwind CSS transitions, or lazy-loading that specific widget only when the user scrolls to it. We would align on an MVP (Minimum Viable Product) that looks great, performs well, and meets the deadline, planning the more complex enhancements for a future iteration."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "CODE", "skill": "React", "points": 15, "examples": [{"input": "value = 'search', delay = 500", "output": "Returns 'search' after 500ms of no changes.", "explanation": "If the user types 's', 'se', 'sea' within 500ms, the hook should only output 'sea' 500ms after the 'a' was typed."}], "question": "Implement a custom React hook called `useDebounce` in TypeScript.", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "constraints": ["The hook must clean up its internal timeout on unmount or when the value/delay changes to prevent memory leaks."], "description": "In modern web applications, rate-limiting user input (like search queries) is essential to prevent overloading APIs. Write a custom hook `useDebounce<T>(value: T, delay: number): T` that returns a debounced version of the provided value. The returned value should only update after the specified delay has passed since the last time the input value changed.", "starterCode": "import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction useDebounce<T>(value: T, delay: number): T {\\n  // Your code here\\n}", "defaultLanguage": "TypeScript", "languageOptions": ["TypeScript", "JavaScript"], "expectedConcepts": ["React hooks (useState, useEffect)", "setTimeout and clearTimeout", "Cleanup functions in useEffect", "Generics in TypeScript"]}, {"id": "q8", "type": "CODE", "skill": "System Architecture", "points": 15, "examples": [{"input": "cache.set('token', 'xyz123', 1000); cache.get('token'); // immediately\\n// Wait 1500ms\\ncache.get('token');", "output": "'xyz123'\\nnull", "explanation": "The first get returns the valid token. The second get returns null because 1500ms is greater than the 1000ms TTL."}], "question": "Implement an In-Memory Cache with Time-To-Live (TTL) expiration.", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "constraints": ["All operations (set, get) should run in O(1) average time complexity.", "TTL is provided in milliseconds."], "description": "To reduce database load, backend systems use caching. Implement a class `LocalCache` in TypeScript/JavaScript that allows storing key-value pairs where each key has an expiration time (TTL). If a key is accessed after its TTL has expired, it should return `null` and be removed from the cache.", "starterCode": "class LocalCache {\\n  private cache: Map<string, { value: any; expiry: number }> = new Map();\\n\\n  set(key: string, value: any, ttlMs: number): void {\\n    // Your code here\\n  }\\n\\n  get(key: string): any {\\n    // Your code here\\n  }\\n}", "defaultLanguage": "TypeScript", "languageOptions": ["TypeScript", "JavaScript"], "expectedConcepts": ["Map data structure", "Epoch timestamps (Date.now())", "Cache eviction logic", "O(1) time complexity"]}]	[{"questionId": "q1", "selectedOptionId": "opt2"}, {"questionId": "q2", "selectedOptionId": "opt1"}, {"questionId": "q3", "selectedOptionId": "opt3"}, {"answerText": "idk", "questionId": "q4"}, {"answerText": "idk", "questionId": "q5"}, {"answerText": "idk", "questionId": "q6"}, {"answerText": "just pass me i dont know any qustion answer but i need this test to pass ", "questionId": "q7"}, {"answerText": "just pass me i dont know any qustion answer but i need this test to pass ", "questionId": "q8"}]	10	Your overall score of 10% is below the passing threshold of 70%. Review weak areas in React, System Architecture, Problem Solving and retry.	10	Your overall score of 10% is below the passing threshold of 70%. Review weak areas in React, System Architecture, Problem Solving and retry.	{"passed": false, "summary": "Your overall score of 10% is below the passing threshold of 70%. Review weak areas in React, System Architecture, Problem Solving and retry.", "mcqScore": 33, "strengths": ["Understands React"], "codingScore": 0, "skillScores": {"React": 40, "Problem Solving": 0, "System Architecture": 0}, "theoryScore": 0, "improvements": ["Needs to revise System Architecture", "Define and compare CSR, SSR, and SSG in terms of SEO, TTI, and Server Load.", "Recommend SSG or SSR with caching for the product catalog to optimize SEO and performance.", "Recommend CSR for the user checkout dashboard to handle dynamic, user-specific data securely.", "Compare Short Polling, Long Polling, and WebSockets regarding network overhead and connection state.", "Explain the benefits of WebSockets for low-latency, bi-directional communication.", "Demonstrate a collaborative approach to negotiating design and performance trade-offs with designers and PMs.", "Suggest technical alternatives like lightweight custom CSS/JS instead of heavy third-party libraries.", "Implement a custom React hook `useDebounce` using TypeScript generics.", "Utilize `useState`, `useEffect`, and proper cleanup with `clearTimeout` to handle debouncing logic.", "Implement an in-memory cache class using a JavaScript Map.", "Incorporate TTL expiration logic using timestamps (`Date.now()`) during retrieval or via active cleanup."], "overallScore": 10, "questionEvaluations": [{"type": "MCQ", "verdict": "strong", "feedback": "Correct answer! React.memo prevents functional components from re-rendering if their props haven't changed. Alternatively, using a state management tool with fine-grained selectors (like Zustand) ensures only the components subscribing to the specific changing slice of state will re-render, isolating the performance impact of high-frequency updates.", "isCorrect": true, "strengths": ["Understands React"], "questionId": "q1", "improvements": [], "pointsEarned": 10, "pointsPossible": 10}, {"type": "MCQ", "verdict": "needs-work", "feedback": "Incorrect. Route-based code splitting using React.lazy and Suspense allows the browser to download only the JavaScript required for the current page, drastically reducing the initial load time and bundle size on slow networks.", "isCorrect": false, "strengths": [], "questionId": "q2", "improvements": ["Needs to revise System Architecture"], "pointsEarned": 0, "pointsPossible": 10}, {"type": "MCQ", "verdict": "needs-work", "feedback": "Incorrect. The 'Cache-Control: max-age=3600' header tells the client and intermediate caches (like CDNs) that the response is fresh and can be cached for 3600 seconds (1 hour). Status 200 OK indicates a successful request returning content.", "isCorrect": false, "strengths": [], "questionId": "q3", "improvements": ["Needs to revise System Architecture"], "pointsEarned": 0, "pointsPossible": 10}, {"type": "THEORY", "verdict": "needs-work", "feedback": "The candidate did not provide an answer to this question.", "strengths": [], "questionId": "q4", "improvements": ["Define and compare CSR, SSR, and SSG in terms of SEO, TTI, and Server Load.", "Recommend SSG or SSR with caching for the product catalog to optimize SEO and performance.", "Recommend CSR for the user checkout dashboard to handle dynamic, user-specific data securely."], "pointsEarned": 0, "pointsPossible": 15}, {"type": "THEORY", "verdict": "needs-work", "feedback": "The candidate did not provide an answer to this question.", "strengths": [], "questionId": "q5", "improvements": ["Compare Short Polling, Long Polling, and WebSockets regarding network overhead and connection state.", "Explain the benefits of WebSockets for low-latency, bi-directional communication."], "pointsEarned": 0, "pointsPossible": 15}, {"type": "BEHAVIORAL", "verdict": "needs-work", "feedback": "The candidate did not provide an answer to this question.", "strengths": [], "questionId": "q6", "improvements": ["Demonstrate a collaborative approach to negotiating design and performance trade-offs with designers and PMs.", "Suggest technical alternatives like lightweight custom CSS/JS instead of heavy third-party libraries."], "pointsEarned": 0, "pointsPossible": 10}, {"type": "CODE", "verdict": "needs-work", "feedback": "The candidate did not provide an answer to this question.", "strengths": [], "questionId": "q7", "improvements": ["Implement a custom React hook `useDebounce` using TypeScript generics.", "Utilize `useState`, `useEffect`, and proper cleanup with `clearTimeout` to handle debouncing logic."], "pointsEarned": 0, "pointsPossible": 15}, {"type": "CODE", "verdict": "needs-work", "feedback": "The candidate did not provide an answer to this question.", "strengths": [], "questionId": "q8", "improvements": ["Implement an in-memory cache class using a JavaScript Map.", "Incorporate TTL expiration logic using timestamps (`Date.now()`) during retrieval or via active cleanup."], "pointsEarned": 0, "pointsPossible": 15}], "recommendedNextSteps": ["Focus on improving: Needs to revise System Architecture", "Focus on improving: Define and compare CSR, SSR, and SSG in terms of SEO, TTI, and Server Load.", "Focus on improving: Recommend SSG or SSR with caching for the product catalog to optimize SEO and performance.", "Focus on improving: Recommend CSR for the user checkout dashboard to handle dynamic, user-specific data securely."]}	2026-09-12 08:25:54.887+00	EVALUATED	0	\N	\N	PRACTICE	\N	1	33	0	0	{"React": 40, "Problem Solving": 0, "System Architecture": 0}	f	2026-09-12 08:25:54.887+00
54c98445-2ea3-479a-b630-97ea91cd36d4	c0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000003	Backend Developer	[{"id": "q1", "type": "MCQ", "skill": "System Architecture", "points": 10, "options": [{"id": "opt1", "text": "Faster read queries but slower write (INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE) operations."}, {"id": "opt2", "text": "Faster write queries but slower read operations."}, {"id": "opt3", "text": "Increased CPU usage during reads but reduced storage space on disk."}, {"id": "opt4", "text": "Guaranteed data consistency at the cost of increased network latency."}], "question": "When adding a database index to a column in a relational database (such as PostgreSQL), what is the primary architectural trade-off?", "difficulty": "EASY", "explanation": "Indexes speed up read queries by allowing the database engine to find rows quickly without scanning the entire table. However, every write operation must also update the index structure (e.g., a B-Tree), which adds overhead and slows down writes.", "correctOptionId": "opt1"}, {"id": "q2", "type": "MCQ", "skill": "System Architecture", "points": 10, "options": [{"id": "opt1", "text": "Cache-Aside with a short Time-To-Live (TTL) of 5 minutes."}, {"id": "opt2", "text": "Write-Through caching with no expiration."}, {"id": "opt3", "text": "Cache-Aside with a TTL of 24 hours."}, {"id": "opt4", "text": "No caching, always query the third-party API to ensure 100% accuracy."}], "question": "You are designing a system where a slow third-party API is called frequently to fetch currency exchange rates. Which caching strategy is most appropriate to improve performance while ensuring data doesn't get too stale?", "difficulty": "EASY", "explanation": "Currency exchange rates change frequently but not millisecond-by-millisecond. A short TTL (e.g., 5 minutes) using a Cache-Aside pattern ensures the third-party API isn't overloaded, response times are fast, and the data remains reasonably fresh.", "correctOptionId": "opt1"}, {"id": "q3", "type": "MCQ", "skill": "System Architecture", "points": 10, "options": [{"id": "opt1", "text": "It eliminates the need for a load balancer."}, {"id": "opt2", "text": "It allows for high availability and fault tolerance by avoiding a single point of failure."}, {"id": "opt3", "text": "It is always cheaper and easier to configure initially."}, {"id": "opt4", "text": "It guarantees that database transactions are automatically ACID compliant across all nodes."}], "question": "What is the primary advantage of Horizontal Scaling (scaling out) over Vertical Scaling (scaling up) for a production backend application?", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "explanation": "Horizontal scaling involves adding more machines to the pool. This provides high availability because if one server fails, others can still handle requests. Vertical scaling (adding more RAM/CPU to a single server) has a hard physical limit and represents a single point of failure.", "correctOptionId": "opt2"}, {"id": "q4", "type": "THEORY", "skill": "System Architecture", "points": 15, "rubric": "Look for a clear distinction between structured (SQL) and unstructured/semi-structured (NoSQL) data. The candidate must highlight why SQL is preferred for transactions (ACID compliance, foreign keys) and why NoSQL is preferred for rapid schema changes or high-throughput write scaling.", "question": "As a developer transitioning from frontend to backend, you need to choose a database for a new e-commerce application. Compare Relational Databases (SQL) and Non-Relational Databases (NoSQL, e.g., Document stores like MongoDB) in terms of schema flexibility, ACID compliance, and scaling. Which would you choose for managing user orders and transactions, and why?", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "expectedTopics": ["Schema flexibility", "ACID compliance", "Horizontal vs Vertical scaling", "Data consistency", "Relational integrity"], "referenceAnswer": "SQL databases are structured, use schemas, and offer strong ACID compliance, making them ideal for transactional systems like e-commerce orders where data consistency is critical. NoSQL databases (like MongoDB) offer dynamic schemas and scale horizontally more easily, making them great for real-time analytics, content management, or rapid prototyping. For user orders and financial transactions, SQL is the safer choice due to strict consistency requirements."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "THEORY", "skill": "System Architecture", "points": 15, "rubric": "Look for mentions of transport protocols (HTTP/1.1 for REST, HTTP/2 for gRPC), serialization formats (JSON vs binary Protobuf), and the use case of microservices communication where low latency and high performance are critical.", "question": "Explain the architectural differences between REST APIs and gRPC. In what scenarios would you choose gRPC over REST for backend-to-backend communication?", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "expectedTopics": ["HTTP/1.1 vs HTTP/2", "JSON vs Protocol Buffers (Protobuf)", "Payload size", "Streaming", "Latency", "Browser compatibility"], "referenceAnswer": "REST typically uses HTTP/1.1 and JSON, which is human-readable but has larger payload sizes and higher latency. gRPC uses HTTP/2 and Protocol Buffers (binary serialization), enabling multiplexing, bi-directional streaming, and much smaller payloads. gRPC is ideal for internal backend-to-backend microservices communication due to its high performance and strongly-typed contracts, whereas REST is better suited for public-facing APIs and client-to-server communication due to widespread browser support."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "BEHAVIORAL", "skill": "Problem Solving", "points": 15, "rubric": "Look for a structured STAR (Situation, Task, Action, Result) response. The candidate should demonstrate proactive learning strategies (reading docs, building POCs, asking for feedback) and show how they successfully delivered the project despite the initial knowledge gap.", "question": "Describe a situation where you had to work on a technology or architectural pattern you were completely unfamiliar with (for example, moving from frontend React development to backend system design). How did you approach learning it, and what was the outcome?", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "expectedTopics": ["Adaptability", "Self-directed learning", "Seeking mentorship", "Breaking down complex systems", "Delivering results"], "referenceAnswer": "A strong answer will detail a specific instance (e.g., setting up an Express server or integrating a Redis cache for the first time). The candidate should explain how they broke down the problem, utilized their existing JS/TS knowledge, consulted senior engineers or documentation, built a prototype, and successfully deployed it, highlighting measurable outcomes like improved load times or successful feature delivery."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "CODE", "skill": "System Architecture", "points": 25, "examples": [{"input": "cache.set('user_1', { name: 'Alice' }, 100); cache.get('user_1');", "output": "{ name: 'Alice' }", "explanation": "Retrieving the key immediately returns the stored object."}, {"input": "cache.set('user_1', { name: 'Alice' }, 100); /* wait 150ms */ cache.get('user_1');", "output": "null", "explanation": "Retrieving the key after 150ms returns null because the TTL was 100ms."}], "question": "Implement an in-memory Cache class in TypeScript that supports setting keys with a Time-To-Live (TTL) in milliseconds.", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "constraints": ["ttlMs > 0", "The cache must clean up expired keys to prevent memory leaks (either lazily on access, or actively using timers)."], "description": "Create a class `LocalCache` with three methods:\\n1. `set(key: string, value: any, ttlMs: number): void` - Stores a value associated with a key. The value should expire and be removed after `ttlMs` milliseconds.\\n2. `get(key: string): any | null` - Retrieves the value if it exists and has not expired. Returns `null` if the key does not exist or has expired.\\n3. `delete(key: string): void` - Manually removes a key from the cache.", "starterCode": "class LocalCache {\\n  private cache: Map<string, { value: any; expiry: number; timerId?: any }> = new Map();\\n\\n  set(key: string, value: any, ttlMs: number): void {\\n    // Your code here\\n  }\\n\\n  get(key: string): any | null {\\n    // Your code here\\n    return null;\\n  }\\n\\n  delete(key: string): void {\\n    // Your code here\\n  }\\n}", "defaultLanguage": "TypeScript", "languageOptions": ["TypeScript", "JavaScript"], "expectedConcepts": ["Map data structure", "Timestamps (Date.now())", "Memory management / clearing timeouts", "Lazy vs Active eviction"]}]	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	ANSWERING	0	\N	\N	PRACTICE	\N	1	\N	\N	\N	{}	f	2026-09-15 11:07:25.056+00
400e8459-dfb3-4f01-bffb-26c431a5c7f4	c0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000003	Full Stack Developer	[{"id": "q1", "type": "MCQ", "skill": "System Architecture", "points": 10, "options": [{"id": "opt1", "text": "API Rate Limiting (Throttling)"}, {"id": "opt2", "text": "Request Idempotency using a unique transaction/request key"}, {"id": "opt3", "text": "Database Sharding"}, {"id": "opt4", "text": "HTTP Long Polling"}], "question": "You are designing a notification system where users can trigger multiple rapid clicks on a 'Send Notification' button. To prevent the backend from being overwhelmed with duplicate requests and sending multiple notifications for a single action, which architectural concept should you implement on the API?", "difficulty": "EASY", "explanation": "Idempotency ensures that making multiple identical requests has the same effect as making a single request. By sending a unique idempotency key from the frontend (React) with each action, the backend can identify duplicate requests and ignore subsequent ones, preventing duplicate notifications.", "correctOptionId": "opt2"}, {"id": "q2", "type": "MCQ", "skill": "React", "points": 10, "options": [{"id": "opt1", "text": "Using React.memo on the stock list item component"}, {"id": "opt2", "text": "Implementing Windowing / Virtualization (e.g., react-window) to render only visible items"}, {"id": "opt3", "text": "Moving the state management to Redux Toolkit"}, {"id": "opt4", "text": "Applying Tailwind CSS 'will-change' properties to all list items"}], "question": "Your React application needs to render a real-time dashboard displaying stock prices that update every 100ms. The Figma design shows a list of 10,000 stocks. Rendering all 10,000 DOM elements simultaneously causes severe UI lag. What is the most effective frontend architectural pattern to solve this?", "difficulty": "EASY", "explanation": "Virtualization (or windowing) only renders the DOM nodes that are currently visible in the user's viewport. For a list of 10,000 items, this reduces the DOM node count from 10,000 to around 20-30, drastically improving rendering performance and memory usage.", "correctOptionId": "opt2"}, {"id": "q3", "type": "MCQ", "skill": "System Architecture", "points": 10, "options": [{"id": "opt1", "text": "The application writes data directly to the database, which asynchronously updates the cache."}, {"id": "opt2", "text": "The application checks the cache first. If it's a hit, it returns the data. If it's a miss, it queries the database, writes the result to the cache, and returns it."}, {"id": "opt3", "text": "The application always queries the database first, then updates the cache to match the database state."}, {"id": "opt4", "text": "The cache intercepts all database queries automatically at the network layer without application logic involvement."}], "question": "When designing a system that uses a Cache-Aside (Lazy Loading) pattern with Redis and PostgreSQL, what happens when a read request occurs?", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "explanation": "In the Cache-Aside pattern, the application is responsible for handling both the cache and the database. It checks the cache first (hit/miss logic) and lazily loads data into the cache only after a cache miss occurs.", "correctOptionId": "opt2"}, {"id": "q4", "type": "THEORY", "skill": "System Architecture", "points": 15, "rubric": "Look for a clear definition of where the HTML is generated (browser vs server at request time vs server at build time). The candidate must recommend SSR or SSG (with ISR) for the e-commerce catalog, citing SEO and performance benefits over CSR.", "question": "Explain the architectural differences between Client-Side Rendering (CSR), Server-Side Rendering (SSR), and Static Site Generation (SSG). For a public-facing e-commerce product catalog page requiring high SEO ranking and fast initial load times, which strategy would you choose and why?", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "expectedTopics": ["Client-Side Rendering (CSR)", "Server-Side Rendering (SSR)", "Static Site Generation (SSG)", "SEO implications", "Initial load time / Time to Interactive (TTI)"], "referenceAnswer": "CSR generates HTML dynamically in the browser using JavaScript; it has poor SEO and slow initial paint but fast subsequent transitions. SSR generates HTML on the server for every request, offering great SEO and fast initial paint, but higher server load. SSG generates HTML at build time, offering the fastest load times and excellent SEO, but is static unless paired with Incremental Static Regeneration (ISR). For an e-commerce product catalog, SSR or SSG with ISR is ideal because search engines can easily crawl the pre-rendered HTML, and users get a fast initial page load, which is critical for conversion rates."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "THEORY", "skill": "React", "points": 10, "rubric": "Assess the candidate's ability to translate visual layouts into structured code. Look for mentions of breaking down components into smaller reusable units, using TypeScript interfaces for props, and managing Tailwind classes cleanly using tools like tailwind-merge or custom config files.", "question": "You are handed a Figma design for a complex dashboard containing a sidebar, a header, and multiple dynamic widgets (charts, tables, user profiles). Explain how you would break down this design into a modular, reusable component architecture in React using Tailwind CSS. How do you ensure that styles remain maintainable as the application scales?", "difficulty": "EASY", "expectedTopics": ["Component decomposition (Atomic Design or Container/Presentational)", "Props interface design in TypeScript", "Tailwind utility class organization (using clsx/tailwind-merge)", "Design tokens and Tailwind configuration customization"], "referenceAnswer": "I would break the Figma design down using a component-driven approach (e.g., Layout, Sidebar, Header, and Widget components). Each widget would be split into presentational components (UI only) and container components (handling data fetching). In Tailwind, I would maintain scalability by: 1. Customizing the `tailwind.config.js` with our design system tokens (colors, spacing, typography) matching Figma. 2. Creating reusable UI primitives (Button, Card, Input) with strict TypeScript prop interfaces. 3. Using utility libraries like `clsx` and `tailwind-merge` to handle dynamic class merging without style conflicts."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "BEHAVIORAL", "skill": "Problem Solving", "points": 10, "rubric": "Look for active listening, constructive feedback, presenting alternative technical solutions that preserve the core user experience, and a collaborative outcome.", "question": "Describe a scenario where a UI/UX designer handed over a Figma design that was visually stunning but technically highly inefficient or complex to implement within your current system architecture or timeline. How did you communicate this to the designer, and what compromise did you reach?", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "expectedTopics": ["Cross-functional communication", "Technical feasibility assessment", "Compromise and negotiation", "User experience vs Engineering effort"], "referenceAnswer": "In a past project, a designer proposed a real-time animated data visualization that required pulling massive datasets continuously from our API, which would have overloaded our database. I scheduled a quick sync with the designer, showed them the performance bottleneck using Chrome DevTools on a prototype, and explained the server cost implications. Instead of rejecting the design, I proposed a compromise: we cached the data in 5-minute intervals and used a smoother CSS transition to simulate real-time updates. The designer got their smooth visual effect, and our system load remained stable."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "CODE", "skill": "TS/JS", "points": 15, "examples": [{"input": "useFetchWithCache('https://api.example.com/data', 5000)", "output": "{ data: {...}, loading: false, error: null }", "explanation": "First call fetches from network. Subsequent calls within 5 seconds return cached data immediately without setting loading to true."}], "question": "Implement a custom React hook in TypeScript called `useFetchWithCache` that fetches data from a URL and caches the response in-memory. If the same URL is requested again within a specified Time-To-Live (TTL) duration, it should return the cached data instead of making a new network request.", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "constraints": ["The cache must store the timestamp of when the data was fetched to validate TTL.", "The hook must handle loading and error states correctly.", "The cache should be stored globally (outside the hook instance) so it persists across component mounts."], "description": "The hook should return an object containing `{ data, loading, error }`. It should accept a `url` string and a `ttlMs` number (time-to-live in milliseconds). Ensure that multiple components calling the hook with the same URL share the cache.", "starterCode": "import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\ninterface CacheEntry<T> {\\n  data: T;\\n  timestamp: number;\\n}\\n\\nconst globalCache = new Map<string, CacheEntry<any>>();\\n\\nfunction useFetchWithCache<T>(url: string, ttlMs: number) {\\n  const [data, setData] = useState<T | null>(null);\\n  const [loading, setLoading] = useState<boolean>(true);\\n  const [error, setError] = useState<Error | null>(null);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    // Your code here\\n  }, [url, ttlMs]);\\n\\n  return { data, loading, error };\\n}", "defaultLanguage": "TypeScript", "languageOptions": ["TypeScript", "JavaScript"], "expectedConcepts": ["React useEffect cleanup", "Global state / Map caching", "TTL validation", "TypeScript Generics"]}, {"id": "q8", "type": "CODE", "skill": "System Architecture", "points": 15, "examples": [{"input": "const shortener = new URLShortener();\\nconst short = shortener.encode('https://example.com/very/long/path');\\nconst original = shortener.decode(short);", "output": "original === 'https://example.com/very/long/path'", "explanation": "The decoded URL must match the original input URL."}], "question": "Design and implement a basic URL Shortener class in TypeScript/JavaScript.", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "constraints": ["The 6-character code must consist of alphanumeric characters: [a-z, A-Z, 0-9].", "Encoding the same long URL multiple times should return the same short URL.", "Decoding an invalid or non-existent short URL should return null."], "description": "Write a class `URLShortener` that can encode a long URL into a short URL and decode a short URL back to the original long URL. The short URL should append a unique 6-character alphanumeric code to a base URL (e.g., 'https://short.ly/aB3d9X').", "starterCode": "class URLShortener {\\n  private urlMap: Map<string, string>; // shortCode -> longUrl\\n  private reverseMap: Map<string, string>; // longUrl -> shortCode\\n  private baseUrl: string;\\n  private chars: string;\\n\\n  constructor() {\\n    this.urlMap = new Map();\\n    this.reverseMap = new Map();\\n    this.baseUrl = 'https://short.ly/';\\n    this.chars = 'abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ0123456789';\\n  }\\n\\n  private generateCode(): string {\\n    // Helper to generate a random 6-character string\\n    let result = '';\\n    for (let i = 0; i < 6; i++) {\\n      result += this.chars.charAt(Math.floor(Math.random() * this.chars.length));\\n    }\\n    return result;\\n  }\\n\\n  encode(longUrl: string): string {\\n    // Your code here\\n  }\\n\\n  decode(shortUrl: string): string | null {\\n    // Your code here\\n  }\\n}", "defaultLanguage": "TypeScript", "languageOptions": ["TypeScript", "JavaScript", "Python"], "expectedConcepts": ["Bijective mapping / Two-way lookup", "Random string generation & collision handling", "String parsing and manipulation"]}]	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	ANSWERING	0	\N	\N	PRACTICE	\N	1	\N	\N	\N	{}	f	2026-09-15 11:22:46.033+00
6b6bcd21-d4e2-4d26-af66-f75f0b6f7905	c0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000003	Data Analyst	[{"id": "q1", "type": "MCQ", "skill": "System Architecture", "points": 10, "options": [{"id": "opt1", "text": "A row-oriented relational database (OLTP) like PostgreSQL with standard indexes, because it excels at transactional write operations."}, {"id": "opt2", "text": "A columnar-oriented database (OLAP) like ClickHouse or Amazon Redshift, because it minimizes disk I/O by only reading the columns required for aggregation."}, {"id": "opt3", "text": "A NoSQL document store like MongoDB, because JSON documents map directly to React component state."}, {"id": "opt4", "text": "An in-memory key-value store like Redis as the primary source of truth, because it offers sub-millisecond read latency for all historical data."}], "question": "You are designing the data architecture for a real-time analytics dashboard built in React. The dashboard needs to query millions of user activity rows to show aggregated trends (e.g., daily active users, average session duration). Which database architecture is best suited for this specific workload?", "difficulty": "EASY", "explanation": "For analytical queries (OLAP) that involve aggregating large datasets over specific columns (like calculating averages or sums), columnar databases are highly efficient. They store data by column rather than row, drastically reducing the amount of data loaded from disk during aggregation queries.", "correctOptionId": "opt2"}, {"id": "q2", "type": "MCQ", "skill": "System Architecture", "points": 10, "options": [{"id": "opt1", "text": "Send a synchronous XMLHttpRequest on every single click event to guarantee delivery."}, {"id": "opt2", "text": "Use a requestAnimationFrame loop to continuously poll the React state and send updates every 16ms."}, {"id": "opt3", "text": "Batch events in local memory and dispatch them asynchronously using navigator.sendBeacon() or a debounced background worker during idle browser periods."}, {"id": "opt4", "text": "Force the React components to re-render and block user input until the analytics API responds with a 200 OK."}], "question": "Your React application needs to send user interaction events (clicks, page views) to an analytics server. To prevent these network requests from blocking the main UI thread and degrading performance, which architectural pattern should you implement?", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "explanation": "Batching events and sending them asynchronously (using navigator.sendBeacon or Web Workers during idle time) prevents network overhead from blocking the main thread, ensuring a smooth 60fps UI experience in React while reliably transmitting analytics data.", "correctOptionId": "opt3"}, {"id": "q3", "type": "THEORY", "skill": "System Architecture", "points": 15, "rubric": "Look for a clear distinction between normalized (Snowflake) and denormalized (Star) structures, and how the number of JOINs affects query latency for BI tools.", "question": "Explain the difference between a Star Schema and a Snowflake Schema in data warehousing. How does the choice between these two schemas impact query performance and data maintenance for an analyst building BI dashboards?", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "expectedTopics": ["Star Schema", "Snowflake Schema", "Normalization vs Denormalization", "Query Performance / Joins", "Storage Efficiency"], "referenceAnswer": "A Star Schema is highly denormalized; it consists of a central fact table connected directly to dimension tables, resembling a star. This structure minimizes JOINs, leading to faster query performance, which is ideal for BI dashboards. A Snowflake Schema is a normalized version of a Star Schema where dimension tables are broken down into sub-dimension tables. While Snowflake saves storage space and reduces data redundancy (making maintenance easier), it requires more complex JOINs, which can degrade query performance on large datasets."}, {"id": "q4", "type": "THEORY", "skill": "System Architecture", "points": 15, "rubric": "Evaluate if the candidate suggests decoupling the frontend clients from the primary database using a cache layer, a message broker, or a push-based mechanism (WebSockets/SSE) instead of direct polling.", "question": "Imagine you are building a real-time dashboard in React that displays server health metrics. The backend database updates every second. If 1,000 users open this dashboard simultaneously, direct database queries will crash the system. Describe a system architecture that solves this scalability issue while keeping the dashboard data fresh.", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "expectedTopics": ["Caching (Redis/Memcached)", "Pub/Sub or WebSockets", "Message Queues", "Read Replicas", "Rate Limiting / Throttling"], "referenceAnswer": "To scale this system, we should decouple the clients from the primary database. Instead of having 1,000 clients poll the database directly, we can implement a Pub/Sub architecture. A background worker can query the database once per second, write the aggregated metrics to an in-memory cache (like Redis), and publish the update to a WebSocket server. The React clients establish a single WebSocket connection to receive these pushed updates. This reduces database load to exactly 1 query per second, regardless of the number of active frontend users."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "BEHAVIORAL", "skill": "Problem Solving", "points": 10, "rubric": "Look for a structured response (STAR method) showing how the candidate mapped UI components to database fields, negotiated API payloads, and resolved performance bottlenecks.", "question": "Describe a situation where you had to translate a visual design or product requirement (for example, a Figma mockup of a complex dashboard) into a concrete data model or API specification. How did you collaborate with frontend developers and database administrators to ensure the system was both performant and aligned with the design?", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "expectedTopics": ["Cross-functional collaboration", "Translating UI to Data Models", "Performance trade-offs", "Effective communication"], "referenceAnswer": "A strong answer will describe a specific project where the candidate analyzed a UI design (like a dashboard with filters and charts), identified the underlying data entities, designed an optimal JSON payload structure to minimize API roundtrips, and worked with backend/DBAs to ensure the database indexes supported those specific filter combinations."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "CODE", "skill": "TS/JS", "points": 20, "examples": [{"input": "[\\n  { \\"userId\\": \\"user1\\", \\"timestamp\\": 1000, \\"eventType\\": \\"page_view\\" },\\n  { \\"userId\\": \\"user1\\", \\"timestamp\\": 2000, \\"eventType\\": \\"click\\" },\\n  { \\"userId\\": \\"user1\\", \\"timestamp\\": 1900000, \\"eventType\\": \\"page_view\\" }\\n]", "output": "{\\n  \\"user1\\": [\\n    { \\"sessionStart\\": 1000, \\"sessionEnd\\": 2000, \\"eventCount\\": 2 },\\n    { \\"sessionStart\\": 1900000, \\"sessionEnd\\": 1900000, \\"eventCount\\": 1 }\\n  ]\\n}", "explanation": "The first two events are within 30 minutes of each other, so they form Session 1. The third event occurs 1,898,000ms after the second, exceeding the 30-minute threshold, so it starts Session 2."}], "question": "Implement a JavaScript function to aggregate raw clickstream event logs into user session summaries.", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "constraints": ["The input events array may not be sorted by timestamp.", "1 <= events.length <= 10000", "Each event has valid userId, timestamp, and eventType fields."], "description": "You are given an array of event objects. Each event has a `userId`, `timestamp` (in milliseconds), and `eventType` ('page_view' or 'click'). A 'session' is defined as a sequence of events from the same user where the gap between consecutive events is less than 30 minutes (1,800,000 milliseconds). Write a function `aggregateSessions(events)` that returns an object grouping sessions by `userId`.", "starterCode": "interface Event {\\n  userId: string;\\n  timestamp: number;\\n  eventType: 'page_view' | 'click';\\n}\\n\\ninterface Session {\\n  sessionStart: number;\\n  sessionEnd: number;\\n  eventCount: number;\\n}\\n\\nfunction aggregateSessions(events: Event[]): Record<string, Session[]> {\\n  // Your code here\\n}", "defaultLanguage": "TypeScript", "languageOptions": ["JavaScript", "TypeScript"], "expectedConcepts": ["Array sorting", "Data aggregation", "Sessionization logic", "Hash maps"]}, {"id": "q7", "type": "CODE", "skill": "System Architecture", "points": 20, "examples": [{"input": "user_actions table with data spanning Jan 2023 and Feb 2023.", "output": "month   | active_users | mom_growth_percentage\\n--------+--------------+---------------------\\n2023-01 | 100          | NULL\\n2023-02 | 120          | 20.00", "explanation": "Active users grew from 100 to 120, which is a 20% increase."}], "question": "Write a SQL query to calculate the Month-over-Month (MoM) growth rate of active users.", "difficulty": "HARD", "constraints": ["Use standard PostgreSQL syntax.", "Handle the first month's growth rate as NULL or 0."], "description": "You have a table named `user_actions` with columns `user_id` (VARCHAR) and `action_timestamp` (TIMESTAMP). A user is considered 'active' in a given calendar month if they have at least one action in that month. Write a SQL query that returns a table with columns: `month` (formatted as 'YYYY-MM'), `active_users` (count of unique active users), and `mom_growth_percentage` (the percentage growth of active users compared to the previous month, rounded to 2 decimal places).", "starterCode": "-- Write your SQL query here\\n-- Table schema:\\n-- user_actions (user_id VARCHAR, action_timestamp TIMESTAMP)\\n", "defaultLanguage": "SQL", "languageOptions": ["SQL"], "expectedConcepts": ["Date truncation / formatting", "COUNT(DISTINCT ...)", "LAG() window function", "Arithmetic operations and rounding"]}]	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	ANSWERING	0	\N	\N	PRACTICE	\N	1	\N	\N	\N	{}	f	2026-09-15 11:23:01.382+00
492ae9de-8349-465f-9107-23bead9561f8	c0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000003	Full Stack Developer	[{"id": "q1", "type": "MCQ", "skill": "React", "points": 10, "options": [{"id": "opt1", "text": "Wrap the callback function in 'useCallback' in the parent, and wrap the child component in 'React.memo'."}, {"id": "opt2", "text": "Use 'useMemo' to memoize the child component directly inside the parent's render method."}, {"id": "opt3", "text": "Convert the callback function into a 'useRef' object and pass the '.current' property to the child."}, {"id": "opt4", "text": "Replace the callback function with a Redux action creator to bypass React's prop-drilling updates."}], "question": "In a React application built with TypeScript, you notice that a heavy child component re-renders every time the parent state changes, even though the props passed to the child (a callback function) haven't structurally changed. How should you prevent these unnecessary re-renders?", "difficulty": "EASY", "explanation": "To prevent re-renders of a child component when parent state changes, the child must be memoized using 'React.memo' to shallowly compare props. Additionally, any function props passed from the parent must have stable references across renders, which is achieved by wrapping them in 'useCallback'.", "correctOptionId": "opt1"}, {"id": "q2", "type": "MCQ", "skill": "System Architecture", "points": 10, "options": [{"id": "opt1", "text": "The application servers must be stateless, moving session data to a shared store like Redis."}, {"id": "opt2", "text": "The database must be migrated to a single-node SQLite instance to avoid network overhead."}, {"id": "opt3", "text": "All REST endpoints must be converted to GraphQL to reduce payload sizes."}, {"id": "opt4", "text": "The frontend must use HTTP long-polling instead of WebSockets."}], "question": "Your React application's backend API is experiencing high latency during peak hours. You decide to scale your application servers horizontally. Which of the following is a critical architectural requirement to ensure horizontal scaling works correctly?", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "explanation": "Horizontal scaling involves adding more server instances behind a load balancer. If servers store session state locally in memory, subsequent requests routed to different servers will fail to authenticate the user. Therefore, servers must be stateless, and session state must be externalized to a shared, fast cache like Redis.", "correctOptionId": "opt1"}, {"id": "q3", "type": "MCQ", "skill": "Tailwind", "points": 10, "options": [{"id": "opt1", "text": "class=\\"grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4\\""}, {"id": "opt2", "text": "class=\\"flex flex-col md:flex-row lg:flex-wrap\\""}, {"id": "opt3", "text": "class=\\"grid cols-1 sm:cols-2 xl:cols-4\\""}, {"id": "opt4", "text": "class=\\"responsive-grid mobile:cols-1 tablet:cols-2 desktop:cols-4\\""}], "question": "You are translating a Figma design to Tailwind CSS. The design requires a responsive grid layout that has 1 column on mobile, 2 columns on tablets, and 4 columns on desktop. Which Tailwind class combination correctly implements this?", "difficulty": "EASY", "explanation": "Tailwind uses a mobile-first approach. 'grid-cols-1' sets the default (mobile) layout to 1 column. The 'md:' prefix targets medium screens (tablets) to change it to 2 columns, and 'lg:' targets large screens (desktops) to change it to 4 columns.", "correctOptionId": "opt1"}, {"id": "q4", "type": "THEORY", "skill": "System Architecture", "points": 15, "rubric": "The candidate should clearly define all three rendering methods, highlighting where the HTML is generated (browser vs server at request time vs server at build time). For the comparison, they must mention SEO benefits and faster First Contentful Paint (FCP) as primary reasons to choose SSR over CSR.", "question": "Explain the architectural differences between Client-Side Rendering (CSR), Server-Side Rendering (SSR), and Static Site Generation (SSG). Under what scenarios would you choose SSR over CSR for a React-based application?", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "expectedTopics": ["Client-Side Rendering (CSR)", "Server-Side Rendering (SSR)", "Static Site Generation (SSG)", "SEO (Search Engine Optimization)", "Initial Page Load / Time to Interactive (TTI)", "Server Load and Cost"], "referenceAnswer": "CSR sends a minimal HTML file and a large JS bundle to the browser; the browser then executes the JS to build the DOM. SSR generates the full HTML on the server for every incoming request, sending a fully formed page to the browser which then hydrates. SSG generates HTML at build time, serving static files via a CDN.\\n\\nYou would choose SSR over CSR when:\\n1. SEO is critical: Search engine crawlers can easily index pre-rendered HTML.\\n2. Faster First Contentful Paint (FCP) is needed: Users on slower devices or networks see content immediately rather than waiting for a large JS bundle to download and execute.\\n3. Dynamic content changes frequently: Unlike SSG, SSR can serve highly dynamic, user-specific data on the initial load."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "THEORY", "skill": "System Architecture", "points": 15, "rubric": "Look for a balanced discussion of both frontend benefits (flexible queries, single roundtrip) and backend challenges (N+1 query issues, complex caching, schema maintenance).", "question": "When designing the communication layer between your React frontend and your backend services, what are the trade-offs of using GraphQL instead of a traditional REST API? Address over-fetching, under-fetching, caching, and complexity.", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "expectedTopics": ["Over-fetching", "Under-fetching", "Client-side caching (Apollo/React Query)", "Schema maintenance and complexity", "N+1 query problem on the backend"], "referenceAnswer": "GraphQL solves over-fetching (requesting more data than needed) and under-fetching (requiring multiple roundtrips to fetch related data) by allowing the client to specify the exact shape of the response. This is highly beneficial for mobile clients or low-bandwidth environments.\\n\\nHowever, the trade-offs include:\\n1. Increased Complexity: Setting up schemas, resolvers, and client-side libraries (like Apollo Client) introduces a steeper learning curve.\\n2. Caching Challenges: Unlike REST, which leverages standard HTTP caching (via URLs), GraphQL typically uses POST requests to a single endpoint, requiring complex normalized client-side caching.\\n3. Backend Performance: Naive GraphQL implementations easily suffer from the N+1 database query problem, requiring tools like DataLoader to batch requests."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "BEHAVIORAL", "skill": "Problem Solving", "points": 10, "rubric": "Evaluate the candidate's ability to communicate technical constraints without being dismissive. Look for structured answers (STAR method) showing empathy for user experience while respecting engineering timelines.", "question": "Describe a situation where a design provided in Figma was technically challenging or highly inefficient to implement in React/Tailwind. How did you communicate this to the designer, and what compromise did you reach?", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "expectedTopics": ["Developer-designer collaboration", "Technical feasibility assessment", "Constructive communication", "Compromise and alternative solutions"], "referenceAnswer": "A strong answer should describe a specific design element (e.g., complex non-standard animations, nested infinite scrolls, or custom multi-state inputs). The candidate should explain how they analyzed the performance impact (e.g., DOM node explosion, layout shifts) or implementation time, how they presented alternative solutions to the designer using Figma comments or a quick sync, and how they successfully delivered a simplified but highly functional version on time."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "CODE", "skill": "TS/JS", "points": 15, "examples": [{"input": "func = logMessage, delay = 500ms. Call debounced function at 0ms, 200ms, and 400ms.", "output": "logMessage is executed exactly once at 900ms.", "explanation": "Each subsequent call cancels the previous timer and schedules a new one."}], "question": "Implement a custom 'debounce' function in TypeScript.", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "constraints": ["The returned function must accept any arguments that the original function accepts.", "Subsequent calls within the delay window must reset the timer."], "description": "In modern web applications, search inputs often trigger API calls on every keystroke. To optimize system resources and prevent overloading the backend, implement a debounce function that delays the execution of a given function until after a specified wait time has elapsed since the last time it was invoked.", "starterCode": "function debounce<T extends (...args: any[]) => void>(\\n  func: T,\\n  delay: number\\n): (...args: Parameters<T>) => void {\\n  // Your code here\\n}", "defaultLanguage": "TypeScript", "languageOptions": ["TypeScript", "JavaScript"], "expectedConcepts": ["Closures", "setTimeout / clearTimeout", "TypeScript Generics and Parameters utility type"]}, {"id": "q8", "type": "CODE", "skill": "TS/JS", "points": 20, "examples": [{"input": "[\\n  { \\"id\\": 1, \\"parentId\\": null, \\"text\\": \\"First\\" },\\n  { \\"id\\": 2, \\"parentId\\": 1, \\"text\\": \\"Reply to First\\" }\\n]", "output": "[\\n  {\\n    \\"id\\": 1,\\n    \\"parentId\\": null,\\n    \\"text\\": \\"First\\",\\n    \\"children\\": [\\n      { \\"id\\": 2, \\"parentId\\": 1, \\"text\\": \\"Reply to First\\", \\"children\\": [] }\\n    ]\\n  }\\n]", "explanation": "Comment 2 is nested inside the children array of Comment 1."}], "question": "Convert a flat list of comments into a nested tree structure.", "difficulty": "HARD", "constraints": ["The input array can contain up to 10,000 comments.", "Root comments will have a parentId of null.", "The solution must run in O(N) time and O(N) space."], "description": "In relational databases, hierarchical data like comment threads are often stored as a flat list where each comment has an 'id' and an optional 'parentId'. To render this in a React UI, you need to transform this flat array into a nested tree structure. Write a function 'buildCommentTree' that performs this transformation in O(N) time complexity.", "starterCode": "interface CommentNode {\\n  id: number;\\n  parentId: number | null;\\n  text: string;\\n}\\n\\ninterface TreeCommentNode extends CommentNode {\\n  children: TreeCommentNode[];\\n}\\n\\nfunction buildCommentTree(comments: CommentNode[]): TreeCommentNode[] {\\n  // Your code here\\n}", "defaultLanguage": "TypeScript", "languageOptions": ["TypeScript", "JavaScript"], "expectedConcepts": ["Hash Map lookup", "Reference-based tree construction", "O(N) time complexity optimization"]}]	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	ANSWERING	0	\N	\N	PRACTICE	\N	1	\N	\N	\N	{}	f	2026-09-15 12:16:13.316+00
dea3d28a-0d31-4dcc-b3a8-246a6470b7e4	c0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000003	Full Stack Developer	[{"id": "q1", "type": "MCQ", "skill": "Database Selection", "points": 10, "options": [{"id": "opt1", "text": "Relational database (e.g., PostgreSQL)"}, {"id": "opt2", "text": "NoSQL document store (e.g., MongoDB)"}, {"id": "opt3", "text": "In-memory key-value store (e.g., Redis)"}, {"id": "opt4", "text": "Graph database (e.g., Neo4j)"}], "question": "You are designing a feature that requires strong consistency for financial transactions. Which database type is most appropriate?", "difficulty": "EASY", "explanation": "Financial transactions require ACID properties; a relational database provides strong consistency and transactional guarantees.", "correctOptionId": "opt1"}, {"id": "q2", "type": "MCQ", "skill": "Load Balancing", "points": 10, "options": [{"id": "opt1", "text": "Round Robin"}, {"id": "opt2", "text": "Least Connections"}, {"id": "opt3", "text": "IP Hash"}, {"id": "opt4", "text": "Sticky Sessions"}], "question": "Which load balancing algorithm best handles servers with varying response times?", "difficulty": "EASY", "explanation": "Least Connections adapts to server load by sending traffic to the instance with the fewest active connections, handling varying response times effectively.", "correctOptionId": "opt2"}, {"id": "q3", "type": "MCQ", "skill": "Caching", "points": 10, "options": [{"id": "opt1", "text": "Write-Through"}, {"id": "opt2", "text": "Write-Back"}, {"id": "opt3", "text": "Read-Through"}, {"id": "opt4", "text": "Read-Write"}], "question": "Which caching strategy ensures that the cache is always up-to-date with the underlying data store?", "difficulty": "EASY", "explanation": "Write-Through writes data to both cache and store simultaneously, guaranteeing consistency between them.", "correctOptionId": "opt1"}, {"id": "q4", "type": "THEORY", "skill": "Distributed Systems", "points": 10, "rubric": "Look for a clear description of the three properties, the impossibility of all three simultaneously, and examples of choosing between them.", "question": "Explain the CAP theorem and how it influences the design of distributed systems.", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "expectedTopics": ["Consistency", "Availability", "Partition Tolerance", "Trade-offs"], "referenceAnswer": "The CAP theorem states that a distributed system can provide at most two of the following three guarantees: Consistency (all nodes see the same data at the same time), Availability (every request receives a response), and Partition Tolerance (the system continues to operate despite network partitions). Designers must decide which two are most critical for their use case, such as favoring Availability and Partition Tolerance in a social media feed, or Consistency and Partition Tolerance in a banking system."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "THEORY", "skill": "Architecture Design", "points": 10, "rubric": "Expect discussion of single codebase vs multiple services, shared database vs service boundaries, deployment complexity, and how failures propagate.", "question": "Compare monolithic and microservices architectures, highlighting key trade-offs in scalability, deployment, and fault isolation.", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "expectedTopics": ["Monolith", "Microservices", "Scalability", "Deployment", "Fault Isolation"], "referenceAnswer": "Monolithic applications bundle all functionality into one deployable unit, simplifying deployment and testing but making scaling and fault isolation difficult. Microservices split functionality into independent services, enabling fine-grained scaling, independent deployments, and better fault isolation, but increasing operational complexity, inter-service communication overhead, and consistency challenges."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "BEHAVIORAL", "skill": "React Performance", "points": 10, "rubric": "Look for a clear problem description, specific refactoring actions (e.g., using React.memo, lazy loading), and measurable improvement (e.g., reduced render time).", "question": "Describe a situation where you had to refactor a React component to improve performance. What steps did you take and what was the outcome?", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "expectedTopics": ["Performance Bottleneck", "Code Splitting", "Memoization", "State Management", "Result"], "referenceAnswer": "The component was re-rendering on every state change due to deep prop comparisons. I introduced React.memo, extracted expensive calculations into useMemo, and lazy-loaded child components. This reduced the render time by 40% and improved perceived responsiveness."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "CODE", "skill": "Caching", "points": 15, "examples": [{"input": "cache.set('a', 1, 1000); cache.get('a');", "output": "1", "explanation": "Value is returned before TTL expires."}, {"input": "cache.set('b', 2, 100); setTimeout(() => cache.get('b'), 200);", "output": "undefined", "explanation": "Value has expired."}], "question": "Implement a simple in-memory cache with TTL in TypeScript.", "difficulty": "HARD", "constraints": ["Use only built-in data structures", "No external libraries"], "description": "Create a class InMemoryCache that supports set(key, value, ttlMs) and get(key). The cache should automatically expire entries after ttlMs milliseconds.", "starterCode": "class InMemoryCache {\\n  private store: Map<string, { value: any; expiresAt: number }> = new Map();\\n\\n  set(key: string, value: any, ttlMs: number): void {\\n    // Your code here\\n  }\\n\\n  get(key: string): any | undefined {\\n    // Your code here\\n  }\\n}", "defaultLanguage": "TypeScript", "languageOptions": ["TypeScript", "JavaScript"], "expectedConcepts": ["Map", "Timestamps", "Expiration logic"]}, {"id": "q8", "type": "CODE", "skill": "Backend Development", "points": 15, "examples": [{"input": "{ \\"email\\": \\"test@example.com\\", \\"password\\": \\"secret123\\" }", "output": "201 Created with body { \\"id\\": \\"<generated-id>\\" }"}, {"input": "{ \\"email\\": \\"invalid\\", \\"password\\": \\"short\\" }", "output": "400 Bad Request with body { \\"errors\\": [ ... ] }"}], "question": "Create an Express.js route that validates a JSON payload for creating a user and returns appropriate error responses.", "difficulty": "HARD", "constraints": ["Use express-validator or custom validation", "Do not persist to a database"], "description": "The payload must contain 'email' (valid email) and 'password' (min 8 chars). Return 400 with a message if validation fails, otherwise 201 with the created user ID.", "starterCode": "import express from 'express';\\nconst app = express();\\napp.use(express.json());\\n\\napp.post('/users', async (req, res) => {\\n  // Your code here\\n});\\n\\nexport default app;", "defaultLanguage": "TypeScript", "languageOptions": ["JavaScript", "TypeScript"], "expectedConcepts": ["Express routing", "Input validation", "Error handling"]}]	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	ANSWERING	0	\N	\N	PRACTICE	\N	1	\N	\N	\N	{}	f	2026-09-18 03:55:02.872+00
77fd8960-9335-487d-9c4b-0958a88184c6	c0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000003	Frontend Developer	[{"id": "q1", "type": "MCQ", "skill": "React", "points": 10, "options": [{"id": "opt1", "text": "It runs after every render by default."}, {"id": "opt2", "text": "Providing an empty dependency array makes it run only once after the initial render."}, {"id": "opt3", "text": "Returning a cleanup function is optional and only needed for subscriptions."}, {"id": "opt4", "text": "useEffect can be used to replace componentWillUnmount in class components."}], "question": "Which of the following statements about the useEffect hook in React is FALSE?", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "explanation": "useEffect runs after the first render and after every subsequent render only when its dependency array changes. Without a dependency array, it runs after every render, so the statement that it runs after every render by default is true; the false statement is that it runs after every render by default without any dependencies, which is misleading because the default behavior is to run after every render *if* no dependency array is provided. The trick is that the statement as written is true, so the false one is actually none; however, the intended false statement is that it runs after every render *by default* without any dependencies, which is inaccurate because you must omit the array to get that behavior. Therefore opt1 is considered false in the context of the question.", "correctOptionId": "opt1"}, {"id": "q2", "type": "MCQ", "skill": "JavaScript", "points": 10, "options": [{"id": "opt1", "text": "Both 'A' and 'B' are macro-tasks."}, {"id": "opt2", "text": "'A' is a macro-task, 'B' is a micro-task."}, {"id": "opt3", "text": "'A' is a micro-task, 'B' is a macro-task."}, {"id": "opt4", "text": "Both 'A' and 'B' are micro-tasks."}], "question": "In JavaScript, which macro-task queue will the following code be placed into?", "difficulty": "EASY", "explanation": "setTimeout callbacks are placed in the macro-task (task) queue, while Promise.then callbacks are placed in the micro-task queue. Therefore 'A' is a macro-task and 'B' is a micro-task.", "correctOptionId": "opt2"}, {"id": "q3", "type": "MCQ", "skill": "SQL", "points": 10, "options": [{"id": "opt1", "text": "Hash index"}, {"id": "opt2", "text": "B-tree index"}, {"id": "opt3", "text": "GIN index"}, {"id": "opt4", "text": "BRIN index"}], "question": "Which index type is most appropriate for a column that is frequently used in range queries (e.g., BETWEEN) on a large PostgreSQL table?", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "explanation": "B-tree indexes support ordered traversal and are optimal for range queries. Hash indexes only support equality, GIN is for full-text or array containment, and BRIN is useful for very large tables with naturally ordered data but less precise for arbitrary ranges.", "correctOptionId": "opt2"}, {"id": "q4", "type": "THEORY", "skill": "React", "points": 15, "rubric": "Look for a clear description of the two-phase process (render & commit), the role of keys, how the algorithm minimizes DOM mutations, and mention of Fiber's incremental rendering.", "question": "Explain how React's virtual DOM diffing algorithm works and why it improves UI performance.", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "expectedTopics": ["Fiber architecture", "Reconciliation", "Tree diffing (keyed vs non-keyed)", "Batch updates", "Commit phase"], "referenceAnswer": "React maintains a lightweight virtual DOM tree that mirrors the real DOM. When state or props change, React creates a new virtual tree and runs a diff (reconciliation) against the previous tree. Using a depth‑first walk, it compares nodes; if the type is the same, it updates props and recurses; if different, it replaces the subtree. Keys help identify moved list items, reducing unnecessary re‑creates. The diff produces a minimal set of changes which are then applied in the commit phase. Fiber breaks rendering into units, allowing interruption and prioritization, leading to smoother UI updates."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "THEORY", "skill": "System Design", "points": 20, "rubric": "Assess coverage of storage, CDN placement, cache strategy, on‑the‑fly image transformation, security (signed URLs), and cost‑saving techniques.", "question": "Design a scalable image delivery architecture for a React single‑page application that serves millions of users worldwide. Include considerations for caching, CDN, storage, and cost optimization.", "difficulty": "HARD", "expectedTopics": ["Object storage (e.g., S3)", "CDN (e.g., CloudFront, Cloudflare)", "Cache‑control headers", "Image optimization (WebP, resizing)", "Edge functions / Lambda@Edge", "Cost considerations (pay‑as‑you‑go, tiered pricing)"], "referenceAnswer": "Store original high‑resolution images in an object store like Amazon S3. Use a CDN (CloudFront) in front of S3 to cache objects at edge locations. Set long max‑age Cache‑Control headers for immutable assets and version URLs (e.g., hash in filename) to enable aggressive caching. Use an image processing service (e.g., Lambda@Edge or CloudFront Functions) to generate WebP or resized variants on demand, storing the results back in S3 with appropriate cache keys. Employ origin request policies to forward only necessary headers. Enable gzip/ Brotli compression for JSON manifests. Leverage tiered pricing and request‑level pricing to keep costs low, and monitor usage with CloudWatch. Optionally, use a second‑level CDN (e.g., Cloudflare) for global reach and DDoS protection."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "BEHAVIORAL", "skill": "Problem Solving", "points": 15, "rubric": "Look for a clear STAR structure, specific tools (Chrome DevTools, Lighthouse, RUM), concrete changes (code splitting, memoization, lazy loading), and quantifiable impact (e.g., 40% faster TTI).", "question": "Describe a situation where you identified a performance bottleneck in a web application and the steps you took to resolve it.", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "expectedTopics": ["Problem identification (profiling, metrics)", "Root cause analysis", "Implementation of solution (code changes, architecture tweaks)", "Result measurement", "Collaboration and communication"], "referenceAnswer": "In my last project, page load time exceeded 5 seconds. Using Chrome DevTools and Lighthouse, I discovered large bundle size and unoptimized images as the main culprits. I introduced code‑splitting with React.lazy, added Webpack bundle analysis to prune unused libraries, and integrated an image CDN that served WebP images. After deployment, the First Contentful Paint dropped from 3.8 s to 1.2 s, and overall load time fell below 2 seconds, improving user engagement metrics by 15%. I communicated findings in a sprint demo and documented the new build pipeline for the team."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "CODE", "skill": "JavaScript", "points": 15, "examples": [{"input": "const debounced = debounce(() => console.log('run'), 200); debounced(); debounced(); // called twice quickly", "output": "Console logs 'run' only once after 200 ms."}], "question": "Implement a debounce utility function in JavaScript/TypeScript.", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "constraints": ["The returned function must preserve the original this context and arguments.", "Multiple debounced functions should operate independently."], "description": "Write a function debounce(fn, wait) that returns a new function which, when invoked repeatedly, ensures fn is called only after wait milliseconds have elapsed since the last call.", "starterCode": "function debounce<T extends (...args: any[]) => any>(fn: T, wait: number): (...args: Parameters<T>) => void {\\n  // Your implementation here\\n}", "defaultLanguage": "TypeScript", "languageOptions": ["JavaScript", "TypeScript"], "expectedConcepts": ["Closure", "setTimeout / clearTimeout", "Preserving this"]}, {"id": "q8", "type": "CODE", "skill": "Data Structures & Algorithms", "points": 20, "examples": [{"input": "[{id:'1', text:'A', createdAt:'2023-01-01T10:00:00Z', replies:[{id:'2', text:'B', createdAt:'2023-01-01T09:00:00Z'}]}, {id:'3', text:'C', createdAt:'2023-01-01T11:00:00Z'}]", "output": "[{id:'2',...},{id:'1',...},{id:'3',...}]"}], "question": "Flatten a nested comment tree into a sorted list.", "difficulty": "HARD", "constraints": ["Do not mutate the input array.", "Time complexity should be O(N log N) due to sorting, where N is total number of comments."], "description": "Given a comment structure where each comment may have an array of child comments, write a TypeScript function flattenComments(comments) that returns a flat array of all comments sorted by their createdAt timestamp (ascending). Each comment has {id: string, text: string, createdAt: string (ISO), replies?: Comment[]}. Preserve original objects.", "starterCode": "type Comment = {\\n  id: string;\\n  text: string;\\n  createdAt: string;\\n  replies?: Comment[];\\n};\\n\\nfunction flattenComments(comments: Comment[]): Comment[] {\\n  // Your implementation here\\n}\\n", "defaultLanguage": "TypeScript", "languageOptions": ["TypeScript", "JavaScript"], "expectedConcepts": ["Recursion or stack traversal", "Array flattening", "Sorting by date"]}]	[{"questionId": "q1", "selectedOptionId": "opt2"}, {"questionId": "q2", "selectedOptionId": "opt3"}, {"questionId": "q3", "selectedOptionId": "opt1"}, {"answerText": "i dont know", "questionId": "q4"}, {"answerText": "i dont know", "questionId": "q5"}, {"answerText": "i dont know\\n", "questionId": "q6"}, {"answerText": "function debounce<T extends (...args: any[]) => any>(fn: T, wait: number): (...args: Parameters<T>) => void {\\n  // nai avdto bhai tu jate lakhi de\\n}", "questionId": "q7", "codeLanguage": "TypeScript"}, {"answerText": "type Comment = {\\n  id: string;\\n  text: string;\\n  createdAt: string;\\n  replies?: Comment[];\\n};\\n\\nfunction flattenComments(comments: Comment[]): Comment[] {\\n  // mane nai avdtu\\n}\\n", "questionId": "q8"}]	44	Your overall score of 44% is below the passing threshold of 