 callbacks are placed in the macro-task (task) queue, while Promise.then callbacks are placed in the micro-task queue. Therefore 'A' is a macro-task and 'B' is a micro-task.", "isCorrect": false, "strengths": [], "questionId": "q2", "improvements": ["Needs to revise JavaScript"], "pointsEarned": 0, "pointsPossible": 10}, {"type": "MCQ", "verdict": "needs-work", "feedback": "Incorrect. B-tree indexes support ordered traversal and are optimal for range queries. Hash indexes only support equality, GIN is for full-text or array containment, and BRIN is useful for very large tables with naturally ordered data but less precise for arbitrary ranges.", "isCorrect": false, "strengths": [], "questionId": "q3", "improvements": ["Needs to revise SQL"], "pointsEarned": 0, "pointsPossible": 10}, {"type": "THEORY", "verdict": "partial", "feedback": "Answer submitted, basic technical review applied.", "strengths": ["Submitted solution attempt"], "questionId": "q4", "improvements": ["Expand on implementation depth"], "pointsEarned": 9, "pointsPossible": 15}, {"type": "THEORY", "verdict": "partial", "feedback": "Answer submitted, basic technical review applied.", "strengths": ["Submitted solution attempt"], "questionId": "q5", "improvements": ["Expand on implementation depth"], "pointsEarned": 12, "pointsPossible": 20}, {"type": "BEHAVIORAL", "verdict": "partial", "feedback": "Answer submitted, basic technical review applied.", "strengths": ["Submitted solution attempt"], "questionId": "q6", "improvements": ["Expand on implementation depth"], "pointsEarned": 9, "pointsPossible": 15}, {"type": "CODE", "verdict": "partial", "feedback": "Answer submitted, basic technical review applied.", "strengths": ["Submitted solution attempt"], "questionId": "q7", "improvements": ["Expand on implementation depth"], "pointsEarned": 9, "pointsPossible": 15}, {"type": "CODE", "verdict": "partial", "feedback": "Answer submitted, basic technical review applied.", "strengths": ["Submitted solution attempt"], "questionId": "q8", "improvements": ["Expand on implementation depth"], "pointsEarned": 12, "pointsPossible": 20}], "recommendedNextSteps": ["Focus on improving: Needs to revise React", "Focus on improving: Needs to revise JavaScript", "Focus on improving: Needs to revise SQL", "Focus on improving: Expand on implementation depth"]}	2026-08-31 09:02:29.779+00	EVALUATED	0	\N	\N	PRACTICE	\N	1	0	60	60	{"SQL": 0, "React": 36, "JavaScript": 36, "System Design": 60, "Problem Solving": 60, "Data Structures & Algorithms": 60}	f	2026-08-31 09:02:29.779+00
81d2c0fd-0b90-4ce5-a4f4-dcd8b3d7024c	c0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000003	Frontend Developer	[{"id": "q1", "type": "MCQ", "skill": "React", "points": 10, "options": [{"id": "opt1", "text": "When you need to memoize a function reference to avoid unnecessary re-renders of child components."}, {"id": "opt2", "text": "When you need to memoize a computed value that is expensive to calculate."}, {"id": "opt3", "text": "When you want to prevent the component from re-rendering entirely."}, {"id": "opt4", "text": "When you need to store mutable state that persists across renders."}], "question": "In a React component, when should you prefer useCallback over useMemo?", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "explanation": "useCallback memoizes a function reference, which is useful when passing callbacks to memoized child components. useMemo memoizes a computed value.", "correctOptionId": "opt1"}, {"id": "q2", "type": "MCQ", "skill": "Tailwind", "points": 10, "options": [{"id": "opt1", "text": "It generates the entire CSS file at build time, reducing runtime overhead."}, {"id": "opt2", "text": "It creates utility classes on demand, resulting in smaller final CSS bundles."}, {"id": "opt3", "text": "It enables server‑side rendering of Tailwind styles automatically."}, {"id": "opt4", "text": "It replaces the need for a configuration file entirely."}], "question": "What is the primary benefit of Tailwind CSS's JIT (Just‑In‑Time) mode?", "difficulty": "EASY", "explanation": "JIT mode generates only the utilities actually used in the source files, dramatically shrinking the CSS bundle size.", "correctOptionId": "opt2"}, {"id": "q3", "type": "MCQ", "skill": "System Architecture", "points": 10, "options": [{"id": "opt1", "text": "Cache-Control: no‑cache"}, {"id": "opt2", "text": "Cache-Control: max‑age=0, must‑revalidate"}, {"id": "opt3", "text": "Cache-Control: public, max‑age=31536000, immutable"}, {"id": "opt4", "text": "Cache-Control: private, max‑age=600"}], "question": "Which caching strategy is most appropriate for serving versioned static assets (e.g., bundle.js?v=1.2.3) from a CDN?", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "explanation": "Versioned assets can be cached indefinitely because the URL changes with each new version, allowing a long max‑age and immutable directive.", "correctOptionId": "opt3"}, {"id": "q4", "type": "THEORY", "skill": "System Architecture", "points": 15, "rubric": "Look for clear definition, benefits (team scaling, independent releases), challenges (bundle size, shared state, UI consistency), and practical mitigation strategies.", "question": "Explain the micro‑frontend architecture pattern and discuss its main trade‑offs for a large‑scale web application.", "difficulty": "HARD", "expectedTopics": ["Independent deployment", "Team autonomy", "Shared UI contracts", "Runtime integration (iframe, module federation)", "Performance overhead", "Consistency and duplication"], "referenceAnswer": "Micro‑frontends break a monolithic frontend into smaller, independently deployable pieces owned by separate teams. Benefits include faster releases, isolated failures, and technology heterogeneity. Trade‑offs involve increased bundle size, coordination of shared UI libraries, runtime integration complexity (e.g., Webpack Module Federation or iframes), and potential UI inconsistency. Mitigations include a shared design system, strict contract testing, and orchestrating loading via a shell application."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "THEORY", "skill": "React", "points": 12, "rubric": "Expect explanation of storing data with timestamps, serving cached data immediately, fetching fresh data in background, and updating UI when fresh data arrives.", "question": "Describe a strategy for client‑side caching of API data in a React application, including how you would implement a stale‑while‑revalidate pattern.", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "expectedTopics": ["Cache key design", "Local storage / IndexedDB", "React Query / SWR concepts", "Stale‑while‑revalidate flow", "Cache invalidation"], "referenceAnswer": "Create a cache map keyed by request URL (or a normalized identifier). Store the response along with a timestamp. When a component requests data, first check the cache: if data exists and is not older than a defined stale threshold, return it (stale). Simultaneously fire a background fetch; when the fresh response arrives, replace the cached entry and trigger a UI update (revalidate). Libraries like React Query or SWR encapsulate this pattern, handling deduplication, retries, and automatic invalidation."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "BEHAVIORAL", "skill": "Problem Solving", "points": 10, "rubric": "Look for a clear Situation, Task, Action, Result (STAR) narrative, emphasis on proactive communication (e.g., shared Figma comments, design tokens), and quantifiable outcome (e.g., shipped on time, user adoption metrics).", "question": "Tell me about a time you collaborated with a designer using Figma and developers to deliver a feature under a tight deadline. What was your role, how did you handle communication, and what was the outcome?", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "expectedTopics": ["Cross‑functional collaboration", "Design handoff and iteration", "Prioritization and timeboxing", "Problem solving under pressure", "Measurable impact"], "referenceAnswer": "A strong answer describes joining a sprint where a new checkout flow needed to be shipped in two weeks. The candidate acted as the bridge, extracting design tokens from Figma, creating reusable Tailwind components, and setting up a quick feedback loop via daily stand‑ups and Figma comment threads. By prioritizing core interactions and deferring non‑critical animations, the team delivered the feature on schedule, resulting in a 15% increase in conversion rate."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "CODE", "skill": "React", "points": 15, "examples": [{"input": "value changes from 'a' → 'ab' → 'abc' within 300ms, delay = 500ms", "output": "'abc' after 500ms of no further changes", "explanation": "The hook waits until the value stabilizes for the delay period before updating."}], "question": "Create a custom React hook `useDebounce` that debounces a value.", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "constraints": ["delay must be a non‑negative integer", "the hook must clean up timers on unmount", "should work with any type T"], "description": "Write a generic hook `useDebounce<T>(value: T, delay: number): T` which returns the debounced value after the specified delay. The hook should update the returned value only after the value has remained unchanged for the full delay period.", "starterCode": "import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction useDebounce<T>(value: T, delay: number): T {\\n  // Your implementation here\\n  return value;\\n}", "defaultLanguage": "TypeScript", "languageOptions": ["JavaScript", "TypeScript"], "expectedConcepts": ["useEffect cleanup", "setTimeout / clearTimeout", "generic typing", "state synchronization"]}, {"id": "q8", "type": "CODE", "skill": "Tailwind", "points": 15, "examples": [{"input": "baseClass='p-2', breakpoints={ sm: 'p-4', md: 'p-6', lg: '' }", "output": "p-2 sm:p-4 md:p-6", "explanation": "Only non‑empty breakpoint values are included, preserving order."}, {"input": "baseClass='text-sm', breakpoints={ xl: 'text-lg', '2xl': 'text-xl' }", "output": "text-sm xl:text-lg 2xl:text-xl", "explanation": "Base class first, then xl and 2xl prefixes."}], "question": "Write a utility to generate responsive Tailwind class strings based on breakpoints.", "difficulty": "HARD", "constraints": ["Supported breakpoint keys: sm, md, lg, xl, 2xl", "If a breakpoint value is an empty string, it should be ignored", "The order of classes must follow the Tailwind default order: base, sm, md, lg, xl, 2xl"], "description": "Implement a function `generateResponsiveClass(baseClass: string, breakpoints: Record<string, string>): string` that returns a space‑separated string of Tailwind classes, prefixing each breakpoint class with its Tailwind screen prefix (e.g., `sm:`, `md:`).", "starterCode": "function generateResponsiveClass(baseClass: string, breakpoints: Record<string, string>): string {\\n  // Your implementation here\\n  return '';\\n}", "defaultLanguage": "TypeScript", "languageOptions": ["JavaScript", "TypeScript"], "expectedConcepts": ["Object iteration", "String templating", "Preserving order of predefined keys", "Handling empty values"]}]	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	ANSWERING	0	\N	\N	PRACTICE	\N	1	\N	\N	\N	{}	f	2026-09-03 06:42:26.806665+00
7a4949df-9bbc-4109-b976-591b134c0445	c0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000003	Full Stack Developer	[{"id": "q1", "type": "MCQ", "skill": "React", "points": 10, "options": [{"id": "opt1", "text": "Hooks can only be called at the top level of a functional component."}, {"id": "opt2", "text": "useEffect runs after every render unless a dependency array is provided."}, {"id": "opt3", "text": "Custom hooks must start with the word \\"use\\" to be recognized by lint rules."}, {"id": "opt4", "text": "Hooks can be used inside class components."}], "question": "Which of the following statements about React hooks is FALSE?", "difficulty": "EASY", "explanation": "Hooks are designed for functional components only; they cannot be used inside class components.", "correctOptionId": "opt4"}, {"id": "q2", "type": "MCQ", "skill": "Tailwind", "points": 10, "options": [{"id": "opt1", "text": "border-t border-red-500"}, {"id": "opt2", "text": "border-t-1 border-red-500"}, {"id": "opt3", "text": "border-t-1 border-red-500 border-solid"}, {"id": "opt4", "text": "border-t border-1 border-red-500"}], "question": "In Tailwind CSS, which utility class would you use to apply a 1px solid red border on the top side only?", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "explanation": "Tailwind provides a specific width utility (border-t-1) for a 1px top border combined with the color class.", "correctOptionId": "opt2"}, {"id": "q3", "type": "MCQ", "skill": "Web Fundamentals", "points": 10, "options": [{"id": "opt1", "text": "POST"}, {"id": "opt2", "text": "PUT"}, {"id": "opt3", "text": "DELETE"}, {"id": "opt4", "text": "GET"}], "question": "Which HTTP method is considered idempotent and safe for repeated calls without side effects?", "difficulty": "EASY", "explanation": "GET is both safe (does not modify server state) and idempotent; repeated calls return the same result without side effects.", "correctOptionId": "opt4"}, {"id": "q4", "type": "THEORY", "skill": "System Architecture", "points": 15, "rubric": "Look for a clear separation of concerns, choice of storage (SQL vs NoSQL), use of a cache for redirects, handling of hash collisions, and strategies for scaling and fault tolerance.", "question": "Design a URL shortener service (e.g., bit.ly). Explain the core components, data model, and how you would ensure high availability and low latency.", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "expectedTopics": ["API endpoints (create, redirect)", "Database schema (hash, original URL, timestamps)", "Collision handling", "Caching layer (e.g., Redis)", "Load balancing", "Horizontal scaling", "Rate limiting"], "referenceAnswer": "A typical design includes a REST API to create short links (POST /shorten) that generates a unique key (base62 hash). The mapping is stored in a durable store (e.g., DynamoDB or PostgreSQL) with a secondary index for analytics. A CDN or reverse proxy (e.g., Cloudflare) serves redirect requests, first checking a Redis cache for the key to avoid DB hits. The service runs behind a load balancer, with multiple stateless instances for horizontal scaling. Rate limiting per IP/user prevents abuse. Data replication and automated backups ensure high availability. Monitoring and alerting complete the design."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "THEORY", "skill": "System Architecture", "points": 15, "rubric": "Expect discussion of faster deployments and team isolation with microservices versus simplicity of monoliths, plus challenges like distributed transactions and monitoring.", "question": "Compare monolithic and microservices architectures for an e‑commerce platform. Discuss the trade‑offs in terms of deployment, scalability, data consistency, and operational complexity.", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "expectedTopics": ["Deployment pipelines", "Independent scaling", "Inter‑service communication", "Data consistency models", "Team autonomy", "Operational overhead"], "referenceAnswer": "A monolith bundles all features into a single deployable unit, simplifying testing, deployment, and data consistency (single DB). However, scaling requires replicating the whole app, and a single codebase can become a bottleneck for large teams. Microservices split functionality into independent services, allowing each to be deployed, scaled, and maintained separately, which improves fault isolation and lets teams work autonomously. The trade‑offs include increased operational complexity (service discovery, monitoring, CI/CD pipelines), the need for eventual consistency or distributed transaction patterns, and higher latency due to network calls. Choosing between them depends on team size, traffic patterns, and the organization’s maturity in DevOps."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "BEHAVIORAL", "skill": "Problem Solving", "points": 10, "rubric": "Look for a clear problem statement, concrete actions (e.g., introduced code splitting, replaced loops with map/reduce), and quantifiable results such as reduced load time or bug count.", "question": "Tell me about a time you refactored a legacy codebase to improve performance or maintainability. What was the situation, what actions did you take, and what measurable impact did it have?", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "expectedTopics": ["Legacy context", "Root cause analysis", "Refactoring techniques (modularization, memoization, lazy loading)", "Collaboration with stakeholders", "Performance metrics"], "referenceAnswer": "The candidate should describe a scenario where a React application suffered from >5 s initial load due to a monolithic bundle. They introduced code‑splitting with React.lazy and dynamic imports, moved shared utilities to a separate library, and added memoization to expensive calculations. After deployment, bundle size dropped by 40 % and Time‑to‑Interactive improved from 5 s to 2.3 s, while the error rate decreased by 30 %. They also documented the changes and ran a knowledge‑sharing session with the team."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "CODE", "skill": "System Architecture", "points": 15, "examples": [{"input": "cache.set('a', 1, 1000); cache.get('a')", "output": "1"}, {"input": "wait 1100ms; cache.get('a')", "output": "undefined"}], "question": "Implement an in‑memory cache with TTL (time‑to‑live) in TypeScript.", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "constraints": ["All operations must be O(1) average time.", "Memory usage should not grow indefinitely; expired keys must be cleaned up."], "description": "Create a class `TTLCache<K, V>` with methods `set(key: K, value: V, ttlMs: number): void`, `get(key: K): V | undefined`, and `delete(key: K): void`. Expired entries should be automatically purged on access.", "starterCode": "class TTLCache<K, V> {\\n  // Your implementation here\\n}\\n", "defaultLanguage": "TypeScript", "languageOptions": ["JavaScript", "TypeScript"], "expectedConcepts": ["Map for storage", "Timestamp comparison", "Lazy eviction"]}, {"id": "q8", "type": "CODE", "skill": "Backend Development", "points": 15, "examples": [{"input": "app.post('/login', validate(schema), handler)", "output": "On invalid payload, response 400 with error details."}], "question": "Write an Express middleware that validates request bodies against a Joi schema and returns a standardized error response.", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "constraints": ["Do not use any global variables.", "The middleware must be reusable for different routes."], "description": "The middleware should accept a Joi schema, validate `req.body`, and if validation fails, respond with HTTP 400 and a JSON payload `{ error: 'ValidationError', details: [...] }`. If validation passes, call `next()`.", "starterCode": "const Joi = require('joi');\\nfunction validate(schema) {\\n  return (req, res, next) => {\\n    // Your code here\\n  };\\n}\\nmodule.exports = validate;", "defaultLanguage": "JavaScript", "languageOptions": ["JavaScript", "TypeScript"], "expectedConcepts": ["Middleware pattern", "Schema validation", "Error handling"]}]	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	ANSWERING	0	\N	\N	PRACTICE	\N	1	\N	\N	\N	{}	f	2026-09-03 06:42:27.184704+00
